{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794468787959,"sessionID":"ses_e3154f688fferuJUyllFRey6r8","part":{"partID":"prt_1ceacb3dd001rqX7Vr6Ped51Qb","sessionID":"ses_e3154f688fferuJUyllFRey6r8","messageID":"msg_1ceac5b3a001oKKt7nihbdW1St","type":"tool","id":"call_01_4igkblrstujaiq5xfotbeqqt","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"{\"openQuestions\":[],\"resolvedQuestions\":[],\"keyFindings\":[],\"ruledOutDirections\":[],\"metrics\":{\"iterationsCompleted\":0,\"openQuestions\":0,\"resolvedQuestions\":0,\"keyFindings\":0,\"convergenceScore\":0,\"coverageBySources\":{}}}\n","path":"specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/research/lineages/opencode-go/findings-registry.json"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/research/lineages/opencode-go/findings-registry.json","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/research/lineages/opencode-go/findings-registry.json"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"{\\\"openQuestions\\\":[],\\\"resolvedQuestions\\\":[],\\\"keyFindings\\\":[],\\\"ruledOutDirections\\\":[],\\\"metrics\\\":{\\\"iterationsCompleted\\\":0,\\\"openQuestions\\\":0,\\\"resolvedQuestions\\\":0,\\\"keyFindings\\\":0,\\\"convergenceScore\\\":0,\\\"coverageBySources\\\":{}}}\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/research/lineages/opencode-go/findings-registry.json\"}"},"time":{"start":1794468787165,"end":1794468787959}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794468787968,"sessionID":"ses_e3154f688fferuJUyllFRey6r8","part":{"partID":"prt_1ceac7108001rmqcmEOA6y3aGL","sessionID":"ses_e3154f688fferuJUyllFRey6r8","messageID":"msg_1ceac5b3a001oKKt7nihbdW1St","type":"tool","id":"call_00_j5fn91wcvwk2zncqb4sjky5q","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"---\ntitle: \"Deep Research Strategy - test and CI bloat\"\ndescription: \"Strategy and progress ledger for the opencode-go research lineage of phase 007 test-and-ci-bloat-research.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"test and ci bloat research strategy\"\n  - \"opencode-go lineage strategy\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: planning\n---\n\n# Deep Research Strategy - test and CI bloat\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nPersistent brain for the `opencode-go` research lineage. Records what to investigate, what worked, what failed and where to focus next. The reducer owns the machine-owned sections and rewrites them after each iteration.\n\n### Usage\n\n- Init: topic, key questions, non-goals, stop conditions and known context are set here.\n- Per iteration: the executor reads Next Focus, writes the iteration narrative, records one iteration event through the append gateway, and the reducer refreshes the machine-owned sections.\n\n---\n\n## 2. TOPIC\nWhich automated tests, CI workflows and git hook checks in this repository are bloat or overengineered, and what should be deleted, merged, narrowed or moved off the commit and push path to shorten test and workflow run time?\n\nRead `specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/spec.md` first for the inventory and CI run times. Subjects: `.github/workflows/`, `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/`, the vitest and node:test suites under `.skilled/skills/*/runtime`, and test files under `specs/`.\n\nSettled and not to repeat: caching, trigger hygiene and workflow consolidation in `specs/system-speckit/repo-operations/002-ci-workflow-efficiency`, and the per-file splits and CI timing in phases 001 to 005 of `specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed`.\n\nFor each proposed cut: the file path, the time it saves, and the regression it would stop catching. Research only: change no test, workflow, hook or source file.\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n## 3. KEY QUESTIONS (remaining)\nGenerated from the reducer registry. Add external or late questions through the research inbox; direct edits are imported as compatibility input and may be replaced on the next reduce step.\n\n- [ ] Which CI workflows duplicate a local hook, another workflow, or a guard that fires no unique regression, and how many minutes does each remove per push or PR?\n- [ ] Which tests under .skilled/skills/*/runtime are redundant, mirror the implementation, or re-assert the framework, and what does each suite cost on the commit and push path?\n- [ ] Which pre-commit and pre-push hook checks cost the most wall-clock time, and which could move to CI or run only on changed paths?\n- [ ] Which test files under specs/ guard retired or closed-packet code rather than shipping behavior?\n- [ ] Which test infrastructure (custom harnesses, serial lists, shared fixtures, helper layers) costs run time or maintenance without proportional coverage?\n<!-- /ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n\n---\n\n## 4. NON-GOALS\n- Re-litigating CI caching, trigger-path hygiene or the workflow consolidation decision settled in `specs/system-speckit/repo-operations/002-ci-workflow-efficiency`.\n- Re-deriving the per-file test splits and CI runner timings already measured in phases 001 to 005 of this packet.\n- Editing, deleting or rewriting any test, workflow, hook, script or source file. This is a research packet; a later phase builds approved cuts.\n- Judging test style or naming where it costs no run time.\n\n---\n\n## 5. STOP CONDITIONS\n- Ten iterations completed (`config.maxIterations: 10`, `stopPolicy: max-iterations`); convergence before the cap is telemetry only and widens the review angle rather than ending the run.\n- Every key question has a cited answer and each recommendation names a path, an estimated saving and the regression it stops catching.\n- A hard stop only if the lineage directory becomes unwritable or the append gateway refuses a record three times.\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n## 6. ANSWERED QUESTIONS\n[None yet -- populated as iterations answer questions]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- MACHINE-OWNED: START -->\n<!-- ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n## 7. WHAT WORKED\n[First iteration -- populated after iteration 1 completes]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n## 8. WHAT FAILED\n[First iteration -- populated after iteration 1 completes]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n## 9. EXHAUSTED APPROACHES (do not retry)\n[Populated when an approach has been tried from multiple angles without success]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n## 10. RULED OUT DIRECTIONS\n[Approaches that were investigated and definitively eliminated -- consolidated from iteration dead-end data]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\n## 10A. SATURATED DIRECTIONS AND DIVERGENCE FRONTIER\n- Completed pivots: 0\n- Failed pivots: 0\n- Audited overrides: 0\n- Saturated: none yet\n- Pivot lineage: none yet\n- Remaining frontier: none recorded\n<!-- /ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:carried-forward-open-questions -->\n## 11A. CARRIED-FORWARD OPEN QUESTIONS\n[Self-owned open questions from iteration write-back -- populated after iteration 1 completes]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:carried-forward-open-questions -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n## 11. NEXT FOCUS\nInventory the commit and push path first: read `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit` and `pre-push`, the package/test scripts they invoke, and map which suites run locally versus only in CI.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- MACHINE-OWNED: END -->\n## 12. KNOWN CONTEXT\n- Spec packet: `specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/spec.md` carries the measured inventory: ~1,270 test files, ~427,643 lines, 32 CI workflow files, and per-workflow wall-clock means (Spec-Kit Check 595s, Deep-Loop Runtime Tests 472s, Skill Advisor Stress Tests 457s, CodeQL 319s, sk-doc Script Tests 279s, OpenCode Plugin Tests 255s).\n- `resource-map.md not present; skipping coverage gate` (config.resource_map_present is false at init).\n- Settled precedent: `specs/system-speckit/repo-operations/002-ci-workflow-efficiency` owns caching, trigger hygiene and workflow consolidation; phases 001 to 005 own per-file splits and CI timing.\n\n### Bounded Context Snapshot\n\n- Source pointers: `.github/workflows/*.yml` (32 files, 3,867 lines); `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/` (pre-commit, pre-push and helpers); `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime` (353 test files); `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime` (186); `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime` (174); `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts` (56); `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts` (54); `.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-md-generator` (36); ~110 under `specs/`.\n- Reuse candidates: the packet's own `spec.md` measured-behavior block, and the predecessor phase timings, so this lineage does not re-measure what is already recorded.\n- Integration points: hook entrypoints invoke repo test runners; CI workflows mirror several local guards (`Naming Standard Guard`, `Command Tree Parity`, `Trigger Index Rebuild`, `Routing Registry Drift Guard`, `Message Contract`).\n- Constraints and risks: hook wall-clock time is unmeasured in `spec.md`, so local-hook savings start inferred until a targeted measurement or a defensible static estimate is found; CI wall-clock includes queue time, so per-workflow attribution must say so.\n\n---\n\n## 13. RESEARCH BOUNDARIES\n- Max iterations: 10\n- Convergence threshold: 0.05\n- Per-iteration budget: 24 tool calls, 10 minutes\n- Progressive synthesis: true\n- research/research.md ownership: workflow-owned canonical synthesis output\n- Machine-owned sections: reducer controls Sections 3, 6, 7-11A, including Section 10A pivot lineage\n- Canonical pause sentinel: `research/.deep-research-pause`\n- Current generation: 1\n- Started: 2026-11-12T07:32:26Z\n","path":"specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/research/lineages/opencode-go/deep-research-strategy.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/research/lineages/opencode-go/deep-research-strategy.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/research/lineages/opencode-go/deep-research-strategy.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"---\\ntitle: \\\"Deep Research Strategy - test and CI bloat\\\"\\ndescription: \\\"Strategy and progress ledger for the opencode-go research lineage of phase 007 test-and-ci-bloat-research.\\\"\\ntrigger_phrases:\\n  - \\\"test and ci bloat research strategy\\\"\\n  - \\\"opencode-go lineage strategy\\\"\\nimportance_tier: normal\\ncontextType: planning\\n---\\n\\n# Deep Research Strategy - test and CI bloat\\n\\n## 1. OVERVIEW\\n\\n### Purpose\\n\\nPersistent brain for the `opencode-go` research lineage. Records what to investigate, what worked, what failed and where to focus next. The reducer owns the machine-owned sections and rewrites them after each iteration.\\n\\n### Usage\\n\\n- Init: topic, key questions, non-goals, stop conditions and known context are set here.\\n- Per iteration: the executor reads Next Focus, writes the iteration narrative, records one iteration event through the append gateway, and the reducer refreshes the machine-owned sections.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. TOPIC\\nWhich automated tests, CI workflows and git hook checks in this repository are bloat or overengineered, and what should be deleted, merged, narrowed or moved off the commit and push path to shorten test and workflow run time?\\n\\nRead `specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/spec.md` first for the inventory and CI run times. Subjects: `.github/workflows/`, `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/`, the vitest and node:test suites under `.skilled/skills/*/runtime`, and test files under `specs/`.\\n\\nSettled and not to repeat: caching, trigger hygiene and workflow consolidation in `specs/system-speckit/repo-operations/002-ci-workflow-efficiency`, and the per-file splits and CI timing in phases 001 to 005 of `specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed`.\\n\\nFor each proposed cut: the file path, the time it saves, and the regression it would stop catching. Research only: change no test, workflow, hook or source file.\\n\\n---\\n\\n<!-- ANCHOR:key-questions -->\\n## 3. KEY QUESTIONS (remaining)\\nGenerated from the reducer registry. Add external or late questions through the research inbox; direct edits are imported as compatibility input and may be replaced on the next reduce step.\\n\\n- [ ] Which CI workflows duplicate a local hook, another workflow, or a guard that fires no unique regression, and how many minutes does each remove per push or PR?\\n- [ ] Which tests under .skilled/skills/*/runtime are redundant, mirror the implementation, or re-assert the framework, and what does each suite cost on the commit and push path?\\n- [ ] Which pre-commit and pre-push hook checks cost the most wall-clock time, and which could move to CI or run only on changed paths?\\n- [ ] Which test files under specs/ guard retired or closed-packet code rather than shipping behavior?\\n- [ ] Which test infrastructure (custom harnesses, serial lists, shared fixtures, helper layers) costs run time or maintenance without proportional coverage?\\n<!-- /ANCHOR:key-questions -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. NON-GOALS\\n- Re-litigating CI caching, trigger-path hygiene or the workflow consolidation decision settled in `specs/system-speckit/repo-operations/002-ci-workflow-efficiency`.\\n- Re-deriving the per-file test splits and CI runner timings already measured in phases 001 to 005 of this packet.\\n- Editing, deleting or rewriting any test, workflow, hook, script or source file. This is a research packet; a later phase builds approved cuts.\\n- Judging test style or naming where it costs no run time.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. STOP CONDITIONS\\n- Ten iterations completed (`config.maxIterations: 10`, `stopPolicy: max-iterations`); convergence before the cap is telemetry only and widens the review angle rather than ending the run.\\n- Every key question has a cited answer and each recommendation names a path, an estimated saving and the regression it stops catching.\\n- A hard stop only if the lineage directory becomes unwritable or the append gateway refuses a record three times.\\n\\n---\\n\\n<!-- ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\\n## 6. ANSWERED QUESTIONS\\n[None yet -- populated as iterations answer questions]\\n<!-- /ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n<!-- MACHINE-OWNED: START -->\\n<!-- ANCHOR:what-worked -->\\n## 7. WHAT WORKED\\n[First iteration -- populated after iteration 1 completes]\\n<!-- /ANCHOR:what-worked -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n<!-- ANCHOR:what-failed -->\\n## 8. WHAT FAILED\\n[First iteration -- populated after iteration 1 completes]\\n<!-- /ANCHOR:what-failed -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n<!-- ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\\n## 9. EXHAUSTED APPROACHES (do not retry)\\n[Populated when an approach has been tried from multiple angles without success]\\n<!-- /ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n<!-- ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\\n## 10. RULED OUT DIRECTIONS\\n[Approaches that were investigated and definitively eliminated -- consolidated from iteration dead-end data]\\n<!-- /ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n<!-- ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\\n## 10A. SATURATED DIRECTIONS AND DIVERGENCE FRONTIER\\n- Completed pivots: 0\\n- Failed pivots: 0\\n- Audited overrides: 0\\n- Saturated: none yet\\n- Pivot lineage: none yet\\n- Remaining frontier: none recorded\\n<!-- /ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n<!-- ANCHOR:carried-forward-open-questions -->\\n## 11A. CARRIED-FORWARD OPEN QUESTIONS\\n[Self-owned open questions from iteration write-back -- populated after iteration 1 completes]\\n<!-- /ANCHOR:carried-forward-open-questions -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n<!-- ANCHOR:next-focus -->\\n## 11. NEXT FOCUS\\nInventory the commit and push path first: read `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit` and `pre-push`, the package/test scripts they invoke, and map which suites run locally versus only in CI.\\n<!-- /ANCHOR:next-focus -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n<!-- MACHINE-OWNED: END -->\\n## 12. KNOWN CONTEXT\\n- Spec packet: `specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/spec.md` carries the measured inventory: ~1,270 test files, ~427,643 lines, 32 CI workflow files, and per-workflow wall-clock means (Spec-Kit Check 595s, Deep-Loop Runtime Tests 472s, Skill Advisor Stress Tests 457s, CodeQL 319s, sk-doc Script Tests 279s, OpenCode Plugin Tests 255s).\\n- `resource-map.md not present; skipping coverage gate` (config.resource_map_present is false at init).\\n- Settled precedent: `specs/system-speckit/repo-operations/002-ci-workflow-efficiency` owns caching, trigger hygiene and workflow consolidation; phases 001 to 005 own per-file splits and CI timing.\\n\\n### Bounded Context Snapshot\\n\\n- Source pointers: `.github/workflows/*.yml` (32 files, 3,867 lines); `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/` (pre-commit, pre-push and helpers); `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime` (353 test files); `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime` (186); `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime` (174); `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts` (56); `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts` (54); `.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-md-generator` (36); ~110 under `specs/`.\\n- Reuse candidates: the packet's own `spec.md` measured-behavior block, and the predecessor phase timings, so this lineage does not re-measure what is already recorded.\\n- Integration points: hook entrypoints invoke repo test runners; CI workflows mirror several local guards (`Naming Standard Guard`, `Command Tree Parity`, `Trigger Index Rebuild`, `Routing Registry Drift Guard`, `Message Contract`).\\n- Constraints and risks: hook wall-clock time is unmeasured in `spec.md`, so local-hook savings start inferred until a targeted measurement or a defensible static estimate is found; CI wall-clock includes queue time, so per-workflow attribution must say so.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. RESEARCH BOUNDARIES\\n- Max iterations: 10\\n- Convergence threshold: 0.05\\n- Per-iteration budget: 24 tool calls, 10 minutes\\n- Progressive synthesis: true\\n- research/research.md ownership: workflow-owned canonical synthesis output\\n- Machine-owned sections: reducer controls Sections 3, 6, 7-11A, including Section 10A pivot lineage\\n- Canonical pause sentinel: `research/.deep-research-pause`\\n- Current generation: 1\\n- Started: 2026-11-12T07:32:26Z\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/006-test-cycle-speed/007-test-and-ci-bloat-research/research/lineages/opencode-go/deep-research-strategy.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794468770056,"end":1794468787968}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794468788840,"sessionID":"ses_e3154f688fferuJUyllFRey6r8","part":{"id":"prt_1ceacba680013t42XRtJD8gOTW","sessionID":"ses_e3154f688fferuJUyllFRey6r8","messageID":"msg_1ceac5b3a001oKKt7nihbdW1St","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"a540f5caaa49255c154d0cd921cd402f5f5b1a37","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":1343,"output":2210,"reasoning":316,"cache":{"read":68224,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794468790474,"sessionID":"ses_e3154f688fferuJUyllFRey6r8","part":{"id":"prt_1ceacc0ca001Xyav6oT3s1VXYq","sessionID":"ses_e3154f688fferuJUyllFRey6r8","messageID":"msg_1ceacba6b001ftUvhjaFiIUhDh","type":"step-start","snapshot":"a540f5caaa49255c154d0cd921cd402f5f5b1a37"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794468793698,"sessionID":"ses_e3154f688fferuJUyllFRey6r8","part":{"partID":"prt_1ceaccac0002ujNgHzI4Hrbtrp","sessionID":"ses_e3154f688fferuJUyllFRey6r8","messageID":"msg_1ceacba6b001ftUvhjaFiIUhDh","type":"tool","id":"call_00_axtt9agboiujj9q7er9am5kt","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled && ls -la .skilled/scripts/git-hooks/ && echo \"=== pre-commit\" && cat .skilled/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit && echo \"=== pre-push\" && cat .skilled/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push"},"output":"\n  # takes the same layout-relative suffix. The packet's own path comes from the layout\n  # module, so a hook installed machine-wide names no spec packet itself.\n  if ! ROUTE_AUTHORED_DIR=\"$(node -e '\n      process.stdout.write(require(process.argv[1]).AUTHORED_PROGRAM_DIR);\n    ' \"$ROUTE_LAYOUT\" 2>&1)\" || [[ -z \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_DIR\" ]]; then\n    echo \"\" >&2\n    echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: could not read the authored program dir from compiled-route-layout.cjs:\" >&2\n    printf '%s\\n' \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_DIR\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n    echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n    exit 1\n  fi\n  ROUTE_AUTHORED=\"$REPO_ROOT/$ROUTE_AUTHORED_DIR/${ROUTE_RUNTIME#\"$ROUTE_SOURCE/bin/lib/compiled-routing/\"}\"\n  for ROUTE_HUB in $ROUTE_HUBS; do\n    # mode-registry.json and hub-router.json are read directly by the hash. Every\n    # SKILL.md under the hub reaches it through the leaves the registry declares, and\n    # so does the ROUTER.md beside each one, which holds that skill's machine router.\n    # A git pathspec glob crosses \"/\", so the last pattern matches a SKILL.md at any\n    # depth. That is wider than the two levels that exist today and is the safe\n    # direction: a hub that grows a deeper mode keeps its manifest minted.\n    # --diff-filter=ACMR excludes a staged deletion, which would otherwise mint from\n    # a tree the leaf has already left and block with a message about the wrong thing.\n    # This set is narrower than the trigger paths in routing-registry-drift.yml on\n    # purpose. That workflow decides whether running the check is worthwhile and can\n    # afford a false positive; this decides what to rewrite and stage, where a false\n    # positive would re-mint on an edit that changed nothing.\n    ROUTE_PATHS=(\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/SKILL.md\"\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/hub-router.json\"\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/mode-registry.json\"\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/*/SKILL.md\"\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/ROUTER.md\"\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/*/ROUTER.md\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/SKILL.md\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/hub-router.json\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/mode-registry.json\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/*/SKILL.md\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/ROUTER.md\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/*/ROUTER.md\"\n    )\n    ROUTE_STAGED=\"$(git_bounded diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR -- \"${ROUTE_PATHS[@]}\" 2>/dev/null)\"\n    [[ -z \"$ROUTE_STAGED\" ]] && continue\n\n    # git runs this hook against a throwaway index when a pathspec narrows the commit\n    # (\"git commit -- path\"), named next-index-<pid>.lock. Anything staged there is\n    # discarded, so the manifest would reach the commit while the real index keeps the\n    # old blob and the next commit silently reverts it. Auto-fixing is unsafe in that\n    # mode, so say so and let the author run the mint themselves.\n    # The name is the whole discriminator, and it has to be: a plain commit runs with\n    # .git/index and \"git commit -a\" with .git/index.lock, which IS renamed into place,\n    # so testing for \"not index\" would block every -a commit in the repository.\n    if [[ \"$(basename \"${GIT_INDEX_FILE:-index}\")\" == next-index-* ]]; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: $ROUTE_HUB needs a re-mint, and this commit narrows its\" >&2\n      echo \"pathspec, so anything staged now would be discarded with the temporary index.\" >&2\n      echo \"Fix: node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-manifest.cjs refresh --hub $ROUTE_HUB \\\\\" >&2\n      echo \"       --skill-root .skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB\" >&2\n      echo \"     then copy the runtime manifest over its authored copy, and commit both.\" >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n\n    # Minting reads the working tree, not the index. If an input is only half staged\n    # the manifest would describe content this commit does not carry, which is a\n    # quieter version of the drift this gate exists to remove. Refuse instead.\n    ROUTE_DIRTY=\"$(git_bounded diff --name-only -- \"${ROUTE_PATHS[@]}\" 2>/dev/null)\"\n    if [[ -n \"$ROUTE_DIRTY\" ]]; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: $ROUTE_HUB has routing inputs staged and unstaged at once:\" >&2\n      printf '%s\\n' \"$ROUTE_DIRTY\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n      echo \"A manifest minted now would describe content this commit does not contain.\" >&2\n      echo \"Fix: stage the rest, or unstage the partial edit, then re-commit.\" >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n\n    ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE=\"$ROUTE_RUNTIME/$ROUTE_HUB/manifest.json\"\n    ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE=\"$ROUTE_AUTHORED/$ROUTE_HUB/manifest.json\"\n    # Fail closed on every precondition. The old shape guarded the copy, the add and\n    # the report behind one existence test, so a missing authored manifest skipped all\n    # three and exited zero with nothing staged and nothing printed.\n    for ROUTE_NEEDED in \"$ROUTE_MINT\" \"$ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE\" \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\"; do\n      if [[ ! -f \"$ROUTE_NEEDED\" ]]; then\n        echo \"\" >&2\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: $ROUTE_HUB cannot be re-minted, missing:\" >&2\n        echo \"  $ROUTE_NEEDED\" >&2\n        echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n        exit 1\n      fi\n    done\n\n    # Captured rather than re-run on failure: refresh writes before it reports, so a\n    # second invocation to show the operator its output would write a second time.\n    if ! ROUTE_OUT=\"$(node \"$ROUTE_MINT\" refresh --hub \"$ROUTE_HUB\" \\\n         --skill-root \"$ROUTE_SOURCE/skills/$ROUTE_HUB\" 2>&1)\"; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: re-minting $ROUTE_HUB failed.\" >&2\n      printf '%s\\n' \"$ROUTE_OUT\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n\n    # The runtime manifest is what serves. The authored copy is what a rebuild would\n    # reinstate, so leaving it behind reintroduces the same staleness on the next one.\n    if ! cp \"$ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE\" \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\" 2>/dev/null; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: could not copy the $ROUTE_HUB manifest to its authored copy:\" >&2\n      echo \"  $ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\" >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n    git_bounded add -- \"$ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE\" \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\"\n\n    # Prove it. An add that silently did nothing leaves the commit carrying a stale\n    # manifest, and no later local gate can see that, so confirm both paths are in\n    # the index carrying exactly what the mint just wrote.\n    #\n    # Ask that question directly, per path: tracked, and no worktree-versus-index\n    # difference. The earlier form counted paths whose staged blob differed from\n    # HEAD, which is a different proposition and wrong in both directions. A mint\n    # that produced no change left both manifests equal to HEAD, so the count was\n    # zero and a correct commit was blocked. Worse, a stale blob already staged from\n    # an earlier run still differed from HEAD, so the count was two and a failed add\n    # passed, which is precisely the case this check exists to catch.\n    ROUTE_CONFIRM=0\n    for ROUTE_MANIFEST in \"$ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE\" \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\"; do\n      if git_bounded ls-files --error-unmatch -- \"$ROUTE_MANIFEST\" >/dev/null 2>&1 \\\n         && git_bounded diff --quiet -- \"$ROUTE_MANIFEST\" 2>/dev/null; then\n        ROUTE_CONFIRM=$((ROUTE_CONFIRM + 1))\n      fi\n    done\n    if [[ \"$ROUTE_CONFIRM\" != \"2\" ]]; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: re-minted $ROUTE_HUB but only $ROUTE_CONFIRM of 2 manifests reached the index.\" >&2\n      echo \"Fix: git add the two manifest paths below, then re-commit.\" >&2\n      echo \"  $ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE\" >&2\n      echo \"  $ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\" >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n    echo \"[gate:route-remint] re-minted $ROUTE_HUB and staged both manifests.\" >&2\n  done\n  fi\nfi\n\n# --- Deep command contract auto recompile (blocking only when it cannot fix it) ---\n# Each compiled deep command contract records a digest of every source it was built\n# from: the deep-loop skill docs and the deep command assets. The doc-version gate\n# above rewrites those docs, so a contract compiled before the commit is stale by the\n# time the commit lands, and CI's contract drift check then fails. Recompile only when\n# a deep-loop source is staged and the drift check reports a difference.\n# Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_CONTRACT_RECOMPILE=1 git commit ...\nif [[ \"${SPECKIT_SKIP_CONTRACT_RECOMPILE:-0}\" != \"1\" ]] && command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  CONTRACT_COMPILER=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/compile-command-contracts.cjs\"\n  CONTRACT_CHECK=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/check-contract-drift.cjs\"\n  CONTRACT_REL=\"${SOURCE_ROOT#\"$REPO_ROOT\"/}\"\n  if [[ -f \"$CONTRACT_COMPILER\" && -f \"$CONTRACT_CHECK\" ]] \\\n     && [[ -n \"$(git_bounded diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR -- \\\n          \"$CONTRACT_REL/skills/system-deep-loop/\" \"$CONTRACT_REL/commands/deep/\" 2>/dev/null)\" ]] \\\n     && ! node \"$CONTRACT_CHECK\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    # The compiler owns the command list, so a fourth deep command needs no hook edit.\n    if ! CONTRACT_COMMANDS=\"$(node -e 'console.log(Object.keys(require(process.argv[1]).COMMANDS).join(\"\\n\"))' \\\n         \"$CONTRACT_COMPILER\" 2>&1)\"; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:contract-recompile]: could not read the command list from compile-command-contracts.cjs:\" >&2\n      printf '%s\\n' \"$CONTRACT_COMMANDS\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_CONTRACT_RECOMPILE=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n    for CONTRACT_COMMAND in $CONTRACT_COMMANDS; do\n      # The compiler prints each file it wrote, one path per line, so those are what\n      # gets staged.\n      if ! CONTRACT_OUT=\"$(node \"$CONTRACT_COMPILER\" --command \"$CONTRACT_COMMAND\" --write 2>&1)\"; then\n        echo \"\" >&2\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:contract-recompile]: compiling $CONTRACT_COMMAND failed.\" >&2\n        printf '%s\\n' \"$CONTRACT_OUT\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n        echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_CONTRACT_RECOMPILE=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n        exit 1\n      fi\n      while IFS= read -r CONTRACT_FILE; do\n        [[ -n \"$CONTRACT_FILE\" && -f \"$REPO_ROOT/$CONTRACT_FILE\" ]] && git_bounded add -- \"$REPO_ROOT/$CONTRACT_FILE\"\n      done <<< \"$CONTRACT_OUT\"\n    done\n    if ! CONTRACT_AFTER=\"$(node \"$CONTRACT_CHECK\" 2>&1)\"; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:contract-recompile]: the deep command contracts still drift after a recompile:\" >&2\n      printf '%s\\n' \"$CONTRACT_AFTER\" | tail -6 | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_CONTRACT_RECOMPILE=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n    echo \"[gate:contract-recompile] recompiled the deep command contracts and staged them.\" >&2\n  fi\nfi\n\n# --- Spec derived-metadata auto re-mint (blocking only when it cannot fix it) ---\n# A packet's graph-metadata.json stores a fingerprint of the spec documents it was\n# derived from, plus fields summarised out of them. Editing spec.md or any sibling\n# doc invalidates both, and nothing local says so: validate.sh reports it, but only\n# for the folder someone thinks to run it against. A repository sweep found 460\n# packets carrying a stale fingerprint, which is what happens when every edit leaves\n# the regeneration to a human who has no reason to remember it.\n# Same shape as the routing gate above and for the same reason: the artifact is\n# derived from the staged documents, so this regenerates and stages it rather than\n# printing a command. It refuses in the two cases where regenerating would be a lie.\nif [[ \"${SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT:-0}\" != \"1\" ]] && command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  # scratch/ holds working files that are not packet documents, and z_archive/ is\n  # retired history nothing should re-derive.\n  SPEC_STAGED_DOCS=\"$(git_bounded diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR -- 'specs/**/*.md' 2>/dev/null \\\n    | grep -v '/scratch/' | grep -v '/z_archive/' || true)\"\n\n  if [[ -n \"$SPEC_STAGED_DOCS\" ]]; then\n    # A doc's packet is the nearest ancestor carrying BOTH graph-metadata.json and\n    # spec.md. Walking up rather than assuming a depth is what makes this work for a\n    # phase child, whose docs sit two levels below the track.\n    # Both files are required because the repository contains grouping directories\n    # that carry metadata without being packets, and the re-derive tool exits non-zero\n    # on them (\"target is not a spec folder\"). Keying on the metadata alone would make\n    # this gate block every commit that touched a document under one of those.\n    SPEC_PACKETS=\"$(\n      while IFS= read -r doc; do\n        [[ -z \"$doc\" ]] && continue\n        dir=\"$(dirname \"$doc\")\"\n        while [[ \"$dir\" == specs/* ]]; do\n          if [[ -f \"$REPO_ROOT/$dir/graph-metadata.json\" && -f \"$REPO_ROOT/$dir/spec.md\" ]]; then\n            echo \"$dir\"; break\n          fi\n          dir=\"$(dirname \"$dir\")\"\n        done\n      done <<< \"$SPEC_STAGED_DOCS\" | sort -u\n    )\"\n\n    # A repository that does not ship the toolchain has no re-derive tool and nothing\n    # to re-derive with, so it passes. Inside one that ships it, a missing tool blocks.\n    SPEC_TOOL=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/repair-derived.cjs\"\n    if [[ -n \"$SPEC_PACKETS\" ]] && { [[ -f \"$SPEC_TOOL\" ]] || _in_toolchain_repo; }; then\n      # git runs this hook against a throwaway index when a pathspec narrows the\n      # commit. Anything staged there is discarded, so the metadata would reach the\n      # commit while the real index keeps the old blob, and the next commit would\n      # silently revert it. The name is the whole discriminator: a plain commit uses\n      # .git/index and \"git commit -a\" uses .git/index.lock, so testing for \"not\n      # index\" would block every -a commit in the repository.\n      if [[ \"$(basename \"${GIT_INDEX_FILE:-index}\")\" == next-index-* ]]; then\n        echo \"\" >&2\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:spec-remint]: spec docs are staged and this commit narrows its\" >&2\n        echo \"pathspec, so metadata staged now would be discarded with the temporary index.\" >&2\n        echo \"Fix: run the repair yourself and commit its output with the docs:\" >&2\n        printf '  node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/repair-derived.cjs --folder %s --apply\\n' $SPEC_PACKETS >&2\n        echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n        exit 1\n      fi\n\n      # Partial staging is checked per packet and up front, because a refusal has\n      # to name the packet and must happen before anything is written.\n      # Re-deriving reads the working tree, not the index. If a doc in a packet is\n      # only half staged the metadata would describe content the commit does not\n      # carry, which is a quieter version of the drift this gate exists to remove.\n      SPEC_ARGS=()\n      # Bash 3.2 ships on macOS and errors on an empty array expansion under\n      # set -u. The guard above guarantees at least one packet, so this is belt\n      # and braces rather than a live path, and it costs nothing.\n      for SPEC_PACKET in $SPEC_PACKETS; do\n        SPEC_DIRTY=\"$(git_bounded diff --name-only -- \"$SPEC_PACKET\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v '/scratch/' || true)\"\n        # Only a half-staged INPUT is a problem: re-deriving reads the working tree,\n        # so the gate's own derived outputs (graph-metadata.json, description.json)\n        # are outputs, not inputs, and a packet dirty only in those re-derives\n        # cleanly. Block only when a real document is half-staged.\n        SPEC_DIRTY_INPUTS=\"\"\n        if [[ -n \"$SPEC_DIRTY\" ]]; then\n          SPEC_DIRTY_INPUTS=\"$(printf '%s\\n' \"$SPEC_DIRTY\" \\\n            | grep -vE '/(graph-metadata|description)\\.json$' || true)\"\n        fi\n        if [[ -n \"$SPEC_DIRTY_INPUTS\" ]]; then\n          echo \"\" >&2\n          echo \"BLOCKED [gate:spec-remint]: $SPEC_PACKET has documents staged and unstaged at once:\" >&2\n          printf '%s\\n' \"$SPEC_DIRTY_INPUTS\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n          echo \"Metadata derived now would describe content this commit does not contain.\" >&2\n          echo \"Fix: stage the rest, or unstage the partial edit, then re-commit.\" >&2\n          echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n          exit 1\n        fi\n        SPEC_ARGS+=(--folder \"$SPEC_PACKET\")\n      done\n\n      # One process for every packet. The re-derive itself is nearly free when a\n      # packet is already fresh, so per-packet spawning was the entire cost: a\n      # no-op packet measures about 1.5s wall against 0.7s of work, and a commit\n      # touching scores of packets paid that startup once each.\n      if [[ ! -f \"$SPEC_TOOL\" ]]; then\n        echo \"\" >&2\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:spec-remint]: re-derive tool is missing: $SPEC_TOOL\" >&2\n        echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n        exit 1\n      fi\n      SPEC_RC=0\n      SPEC_OUT=\"$(node \"$SPEC_TOOL\" \\\n            ${SPEC_ARGS[@]+\"${SPEC_ARGS[@]}\"} --apply 2>&1)\" || SPEC_RC=$?\n\n      # One call now covers every packet, so a failure can leave earlier packets\n      # already rewritten on disk. Leaving those unstaged makes the next attempt\n      # refuse a packet that is not the problem, reporting a half-staged tree\n      # instead of the real failure. Stage what succeeded either way, then decide.\n      # Which packets the tool actually rewrote has to be decided BEFORE staging,\n      # because staging is what clears the unstaged-diff signal that answers it.\n      SPEC_WROTE=()\n      for SPEC_PACKET in $SPEC_PACKETS; do\n        SPEC_PAIR=(\"$SPEC_PACKET/graph-metadata.json\" \"$SPEC_PACKET/description.json\")\n        SPEC_CHANGED=\"$(git_bounded diff --name-only -- \"${SPEC_PAIR[@]}\" 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n        [[ -z \"$SPEC_CHANGED\" ]] && continue\n        SPEC_WROTE+=(\"$SPEC_PACKET\")\n        git_bounded add -- \"${SPEC_PAIR[@]}\" 2>/dev/null || true\n      done\n\n      if [[ \"$SPEC_RC\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n        echo \"\" >&2\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:spec-remint]: could not re-derive metadata for the staged packets:\" >&2\n        printf '%s\\n' \"$SPEC_OUT\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n        echo \"Any packet the tool did repair has been staged, so a retry reports this\" >&2\n        echo \"same failure rather than a half-written tree.\" >&2\n        echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n        exit 1\n      fi\n\n      # Confirmation stays per packet so a path that fails to reach the index is\n      # named. Nothing to stage is the common case: most doc edits leave the\n      # derived fields identical, and the tool is a no-op then, so only the\n      # packets recorded above are confirmed and reported.\n      for SPEC_PACKET in ${SPEC_WROTE[@]+\"${SPEC_WROTE[@]}\"}; do\n        SPEC_PAIR=(\"$SPEC_PACKET/graph-metadata.json\" \"$SPEC_PACKET/description.json\")\n\n        # Prove it. An add that silently did nothing leaves the commit carrying stale\n        # metadata, and no later local gate can see that.\n        SPEC_LEFT=\"$(git_bounded diff --name-only -- \"${SPEC_PAIR[@]}\" 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n        if [[ -n \"$SPEC_LEFT\" ]]; then\n          echo \"\" >&2\n          echo \"BLOCKED [gate:spec-remint]: re-derived $SPEC_PACKET but this did not reach the index:\" >&2\n          printf '%s\\n' \"$SPEC_LEFT\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n          echo \"Fix: git add those paths, then re-commit.\" >&2\n          echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n          exit 1\n        fi\n        echo \"[gate:spec-remint] re-derived $SPEC_PACKET and staged its metadata.\" >&2\n      done\n    fi\n  fi\nfi\n\n# --- Tool ownership map gate (blocking) ---\n# Blocks commits when the committed generated map no longer matches TOOL_DEFINITIONS.\n# Manual-testing-playbook operator contract: enforced by CI only (pull request,\n# push to main, and a nightly cron), which is broader than this copy could be.\n\nexit 0\n=== pre-push\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Pre-push hook — independent safety and consistency gates run before a push:\n#\n#   Mass deletion:     blocks a push that deletes more tracked files than\n#                      SPECKIT_MASS_DELETION_THRESHOLD (default 100);\n#                      SPECKIT_ALLOW_MASS_DELETION=1 authorizes one push.\n#   Permission:        ANY push (new branch OR update) to a branch outside\n#                      the remote allowlist needs an explicit ask-first\n#                      confirmation for THIS push specifically — a prior\n#                      approval for an earlier push does not carry forward.\n#                      Release branches (skilled/v*) are exempt, and the\n#                      live-branch autosync publish (SPECKIT_AUTOSYNC=1 with\n#                      SPECKIT_LIVE_BRANCH=<branch>) passes for updates to\n#                      that one branch.\n#   Skill metadata:    warns when skill changes leave root metadata stale;\n#                      never blocks (SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_SKILL_GATE=1 skips).\n#   Compiled routing:  the route guard must pass, and a pushed commit that is\n#                      HEAD must carry the routing bytes the guard approved\n#                      (SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE=1 skips both).\n#   Track roots:       each pushed ref's tip must have track roots that list\n#                      exactly the packets they hold\n#                      (SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_TRACK_GATE=1 skips).\n#   Message contract:  every commit the push adds and every new branch name\n#                      must meet the rules the repository's own templates\n#                      declare. This is what catches a commit made with\n#                      --no-verify, and it has no bypass.\n#   Commit-Id:         in every repository lib/commit-id-policy.sh covers,\n#                      each non-merge commit the push adds must carry a\n#                      Commit-Id; no bypass, but git config --local\n#                      skilled.commitId false exempts the repository.\n#\n# The gates are independently bypassable; skipping one never skips another.\n# The hooks are installed machine-wide. Scripts from a repository's own tree run\n# only in the checkout the hooks live in (or one of its worktrees), or where the\n# repository's local config sets skilled.trustRepoHooks=true.\n#\n# Protocol: git feeds \"<local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>\"\n# lines on stdin, one per ref being pushed. A remote sha of all-zeros means\n# the push CREATES a new remote branch.\n#\n# Bypass permission only: SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=1 git push ...\n# Allowlist file:         .skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/remote-branch-allowlist.txt\n# Install:   bash .skilled/scripts/install-git-hooks.sh\n# Uninstall: bash .skilled/scripts/install-git-hooks.sh --uninstall\n\nset -euo pipefail\n\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || true)\"\nif [[ -z \"$REPO_ROOT\" ]]; then\n  exit 0\nfi\n\n# >>> source-root selection (identical in every hook script)\n# The source tree sits under .skilled or .opencode, and a checkout may carry both, one,\n# or an empty placeholder of either. The authored sentinel inside the tree decides,\n# never a directory existing, and .skilled wins when both carry it. These scripts run\n# for every repository on the machine, so a missing file means either this repository\n# ships no toolchain or its install is broken, and the same sentinel tells them apart.\nSOURCE_ROOT=\"$REPO_ROOT/.skilled\"\n[[ -f \"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md\" ]] || SOURCE_ROOT=\"$REPO_ROOT/.opencode\"\n_in_toolchain_repo() {\n  [[ -n \"$REPO_ROOT\" && -f \"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md\" ]]\n}\n# <<< source-root selection\n# >>> hook trust (identical in every global hook script)\n# These scripts are installed once for every repository on the machine, and the block\n# above picks the source tree from the repository the hook fires in. A cloned repository\n# controls that tree, so its scripts run only when it shares a git common dir with the\n# checkout these hooks live in (that checkout or one of its worktrees), or when its local\n# config sets skilled.trustRepoHooks=true. Local config is never cloned, and command-line\n# or environment config does not count, so a repository cannot opt itself in. Trust\n# covers the repository, not a branch: any branch checked out in a trusted checkout or\n# its worktrees runs its own tree's scripts, so review an untrusted branch in a separate\n# clone. An untrusted repository is treated as one without the toolchain.\n_trust_self=\"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\"\nwhile [[ -L \"$_trust_self\" ]]; do\n  _trust_link=\"$(readlink \"$_trust_self\")\"\n  case \"$_trust_link\" in /*) _trust_self=\"$_trust_link\" ;; *) _trust_self=\"$(dirname \"$_trust_self\")/$_trust_link\" ;; esac\ndone\n_hook_common=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$_trust_self\")\" 2>/dev/null && env -u GIT_DIR -u GIT_WORK_TREE -u GIT_COMMON_DIR -u GIT_INDEX_FILE git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n_repo_common=\"$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null || true)\"\nif [[ -n \"$REPO_ROOT\" && ( -z \"$_hook_common\" || \"$_hook_common\" != \"$_repo_common\" ) ]] \\\n  && [[ \"$(git config --local --bool skilled.trustRepoHooks 2>/dev/null || true)\" != \"true\" ]]; then\n  # /dev/null is never a directory, so no repository can ship files under this root.\n  SOURCE_ROOT=\"/dev/null/.skilled-untrusted\"\nfi\n# <<< hook trust\n\n# Every git call below that reads the index or the working tree goes through\n# git_bounded: fsmonitor off and a wall-clock bound, so a wedged daemon cannot hang a\n# push. A timeout is recorded and the exit trap turns it into a refusal, whatever the\n# gate that made the call would have decided with an empty answer. Without the helper\n# the calls run unbounded, as before, with fsmonitor still off.\n_bounded_git_lib=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$_trust_self\")\" && pwd -P)/lib/bounded-git.sh\"\nif [[ -r \"$_bounded_git_lib\" ]]; then\n  # shellcheck source=lib/bounded-git.sh\n  . \"$_bounded_git_lib\"\n  git_bound_arm\nelse\n  echo \"WARNING [gate:git-timeout]: $_bounded_git_lib is missing, so git calls here run without a time bound.\" >&2\n  git_bounded() { git -c core.fsmonitor=false \"$@\"; }\n  git_bound_finish() { exit \"$1\"; }\nfi\n_pre_push_exit() {\n  local rc=$?\n  git_bound_finish \"$rc\"\n}\ntrap _pre_push_exit EXIT\n\n# Mass-deletion guard: source if present. The gate inside the loop checks that\n# its function actually loaded before using it, so a lib that fails to load means\n# the deletion ceiling is not enforced (fail-open). A lib missing from a checkout\n# that ships the toolchain is a broken install instead, and the gate then blocks\n# each update push until SPECKIT_ALLOW_MASS_DELETION=1 approves it.\n_MASS_DEL_GUARD=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/scripts/git-hooks/lib/mass-deletion-guard.sh\"\n_MASS_DEL_MISSING=0\nif [[ -f \"$_MASS_DEL_GUARD\" ]]; then\n  set +e\n  # shellcheck source=/dev/null\n  . \"$_MASS_DEL_GUARD\" 2>/dev/null\n  _mass_guard_rc=$?\n  set -e\n  if [[ \"$_mass_guard_rc\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n    echo \"WARNING [gate:mass-deletion]: guard unavailable; push gate failed open.\" >&2\n  fi\nelif _in_toolchain_repo; then\n  _MASS_DEL_MISSING=1\nfi\n\n# --- Validator load: a broken validator never blocks a push, and a missing one ---\n# --- blocks only in a checkout that ships the toolchain ---\nNAMING=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh\"\nNAMING_AVAILABLE=1\nNAMING_MISSING=0\nif [[ ! -f \"$NAMING\" ]]; then\n  # Without the script the remote allowlist cannot be read, so the permission gate\n  # below asks for approval rather than disappearing. A repository without the\n  # toolchain has no allowlist to read and gets no new output from these hooks.\n  if _in_toolchain_repo; then\n    NAMING_MISSING=1\n  fi\n  NAMING_AVAILABLE=0\nelse\n  # A broken validator must not suppress the independent metadata and test gates.\n  set +e\n  # shellcheck source=/dev/null\n  source \"$NAMING\" 2>/dev/null\n  _src_rc=$?\n  set -e\n  if [[ \"$_src_rc\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n    echo \"⚠️  pre-push: failed to source worktree-naming.sh — skipping the remote-push-permission gate.\" >&2\n    NAMING_AVAILABLE=0\n  elif ! command -v is_remote_push_allowlisted >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    echo \"⚠️  pre-push: worktree-naming.sh loaded without is_remote_push_allowlisted — skipping the remote-push-permission gate.\" >&2\n    NAMING_AVAILABLE=0\n  fi\nfi\n\n\n\n# --- Message contract: the rules come from the pushed repository's templates, ---\n# --- the validator from beside this hook ---\nREMOTE_NAME=\"${1:-origin}\"\n_self=\"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\"\nwhile [[ -L \"$_self\" ]]; do\n  _link=\"$(readlink \"$_self\")\"\n  case \"$_link\" in /*) _self=\"$_link\" ;; *) _self=\"$(dirname \"$_self\")/$_link\" ;; esac\ndone\nHOOK_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$_self\")\" && pwd -P)\"\n# Gate settings: a gate the operator switched off in git config (speckit.hooks.<key>)\n# runs as if its SPECKIT_SKIP_* variable were set. Without the helper every gate runs.\n# Other repositories run none of these gates, so they read no setting and print nothing.\nif _in_toolchain_repo && [[ -r \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/gate-config.sh\" ]]; then\n  # shellcheck source=lib/gate-config.sh\n  . \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/gate-config.sh\"\n  gate_config_apply pre-push\nfi\nCONTRACT_GATE_READY=0\nCONTRACT_GATE_BROKEN=\"\"\nif [[ -f \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/message-contract-gate.sh\" ]]; then\n  # shellcheck source=lib/message-contract-gate.sh\n  . \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/message-contract-gate.sh\"\n  CONTRACT_VALIDATOR=\"$(mcg_validator_path \"$HOOK_DIR\" \"$REPO_ROOT\")\"\n  _unavailable=\"$(mcg_unavailable_reason \"$CONTRACT_VALIDATOR\")\"\n  if [[ -z \"$_unavailable\" ]]; then\n    CONTRACT_GATE_READY=1\n  elif mcg_repo_declares_rules \"$REPO_ROOT\" commit-message-template.md \\\n       || mcg_repo_declares_rules \"$REPO_ROOT\" worktree-checklist.md; then\n    CONTRACT_GATE_BROKEN=\"$_unavailable\"\n  fi\nelif _in_toolchain_repo; then\n  CONTRACT_GATE_BROKEN=\"the hook helpers are missing at $HOOK_DIR/lib\"\nfi\n\n# --- Commit-Id presence: the policy comes from beside this hook, never from the ---\n# --- pushed repository, so the trust gate cannot switch it off ---\nCOMMIT_ID_GATE=0\nCOMMIT_ID_BROKEN=0\nif [[ -f \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/commit-id-policy.sh\" ]]; then\n  # shellcheck source=lib/commit-id-policy.sh\n  . \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/commit-id-policy.sh\"\n  if cip_repo_wants_ids \"$REPO_ROOT\"; then\n    COMMIT_ID_GATE=1\n  fi\nelif _in_toolchain_repo; then\n  COMMIT_ID_BROKEN=1\nfi\n\nREJECTED=0\nAUTOSYNC_LIVE_PUSH=0\nSKILL_CHANGES_PUSHED=0\nPUSHED_SHAS=\"\"\n\nwhile read -r _ local_sha remote_ref remote_sha; do\n  [[ -n \"$remote_ref\" ]] || continue\n\n  case \"$remote_ref\" in\n    refs/heads/*) branch_name=\"${remote_ref#refs/heads/}\" ;;\n    *) continue ;;   # tags and other ref types sit outside both gates\n  esac\n\n  is_autosync_live=0\n  if [[ \"${SPECKIT_AUTOSYNC:-0}\" = \"1\" && -n \"${SPECKIT_LIVE_BRANCH:-}\" && \"$branch_name\" = \"$SPECKIT_LIVE_BRANCH\" ]]; then\n    is_autosync_live=1\n    AUTOSYNC_LIVE_PUSH=1\n  fi\n\n  is_new=0\n  if [[ \"$remote_sha\" =~ ^0+$ ]]; then\n    is_new=1\n  fi\n\n  # ── Gate 0: mass-deletion ceiling — runs for EVERY branch, releases included.\n  # The destructive stale-tree push that motivated this guard went to a release\n  # branch, which the naming and permission gates deliberately skip; this gate\n  # must not. Updates only: a brand-new branch has no prior remote state to\n  # delete from. Fail-open if the guard lib did not load.\n  if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 && \"$_MASS_DEL_MISSING\" -eq 1 && \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_MASS_DELETION:-0}\" != \"1\" ]]; then\n    echo \"BLOCKED [gate:mass-deletion]: guard library is missing: $_MASS_DEL_GUARD\" >&2\n    echo \"Approve this push once, deliberately: SPECKIT_ALLOW_MASS_DELETION=1 git push ...\" >&2\n    REJECTED=1\n    continue\n  fi\n  if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 ]] && command -v mass_deletion_verdict >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    _mdg_pushdel=\"$(mass_deletion_range_count \"$remote_sha\" \"$local_sha\")\"\n    if ! mass_deletion_verdict \"$_mdg_pushdel\"; then\n      mass_deletion_report push \"$_mdg_pushdel\" \"($branch_name $remote_sha..$local_sha)\"\n      # An autosync publish to the live branch needs a different explanation:\n      # the operator is not at the keyboard, so the generic stale-tree message\n      # reads as an unexpected hook failure. Name the mass-deletion ceiling as\n      # the cause and point at the manual publish path for the session's work.\n      if [[ \"$is_autosync_live\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n        echo \"AUTOSYNC BLOCKED [gate:mass-deletion]: live branch '$branch_name'.\" >&2\n        echo \"   The mass-deletion ceiling (${SPECKIT_MASS_DELETION_THRESHOLD:-100} tracked files) blocked it.\" >&2\n        echo \"   A session likely committed against a stale tree. Inspect the range, then publish manually.\" >&2\n        echo \"   Manual publish:  bash .skilled/bin/git-sync.sh --live $branch_name\" >&2\n      fi\n      REJECTED=1\n      continue\n    fi\n  fi\n\n  if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 ]] && ! git_bounded -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" diff --quiet \"$remote_sha\" \"$local_sha\" -- .opencode/skills .skilled/skills 2>/dev/null; then\n    SKILL_CHANGES_PUSHED=1\n  fi\n\n  PUSHED_SHAS=\"${PUSHED_SHAS} ${local_sha}\"\n\n  # ── Gate 6: message contract — every branch, releases and main included ──\n  # A deletion pushes no commits. Only what this push adds is checked: commits that\n  # no remote-tracking ref holds, of any remote, since the target may be a URL or a\n  # remote never fetched, and for an update nothing the old remote tip holds. An old\n  # tip missing from the local object store (a force-push over unfetched work) cannot\n  # be excluded, so the remote-tracking refs bound the range alone. The ref being\n  # overwritten is excluded from the Commit-Id check so a rebase is not compared\n  # with itself.\n  if [[ ! \"$local_sha\" =~ ^0+$ ]]; then\n    if [[ -n \"$CONTRACT_GATE_BROKEN\" ]]; then\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:message-contract]: this repository declares message rules, but $CONTRACT_GATE_BROKEN.\" >&2\n      REJECTED=1\n      continue\n    fi\n    if [[ \"$CONTRACT_GATE_READY\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n      _range=\"$local_sha --not --remotes\"\n      if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 ]] && git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" cat-file -e \"${remote_sha}^{commit}\" 2>/dev/null; then\n        _range=\"$local_sha --not $remote_sha --remotes\"\n      fi\n      _contract_rc=0\n      node \"$CONTRACT_VALIDATOR\" --repo \"$REPO_ROOT\" --rev-list \"$_range\" \\\n        --exclude-ref \"refs/remotes/$REMOTE_NAME/$branch_name\" || _contract_rc=$?\n      if [[ \"$_contract_rc\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:message-contract]: commits pushed to '$branch_name' break the commit rules above.\" >&2\n        echo \"   Reword them (git rebase -i, then reword) and push again.\" >&2\n        REJECTED=1\n        continue\n      elif [[ \"$_contract_rc\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:message-contract]: the message validator could not check the commits pushed to '$branch_name' (exit $_contract_rc); its error is above.\" >&2\n        REJECTED=1\n        continue\n      fi\n      if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 1 ]] && ! node \"$CONTRACT_VALIDATOR\" --repo \"$REPO_ROOT\" --branch \"$branch_name\"; then\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:message-contract]: new branch '$branch_name' breaks the naming rules above.\" >&2\n        REJECTED=1\n        continue\n      fi\n    fi\n  fi\n\n  # ── Gate 7: Commit-Id presence, every branch, no bypass ──\n  # Catches what commit-msg cannot: a commit made with --no-verify or by a tool\n  # that runs no hooks. The range is Gate 6's, and merges are exempt as in the\n  # stamper. git config --local skilled.commitId false exempts the repository.\n  if [[ ! \"$local_sha\" =~ ^0+$ ]]; then\n    if [[ \"$COMMIT_ID_BROKEN\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:commit-id]: the hook helpers are missing at $HOOK_DIR/lib; re-run: bash .skilled/scripts/install-git-hooks.sh\" >&2\n      REJECTED=1\n      continue\n    fi\n    # git only offers a local sha it holds, so one that does not resolve has nothing to check.\n    if [[ \"$COMMIT_ID_GATE\" -eq 1 ]] && git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" cat-file -e \"${local_sha}^{commit}\" 2>/dev/null; then\n      _id_range=(\"$local_sha\" --not --remotes)\n      if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 ]] && git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" cat-file -e \"${remote_sha}^{commit}\" 2>/dev/null; then\n        _id_range=(\"$local_sha\" --not \"$remote_sha\" --remotes)\n      fi\n      _id_log=\"\"\n      if ! _id_log=\"$(git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" log --no-merges \\\n           --format='%h%x09%(trailers:key=Commit-Id,valueonly,separator=%x2C)%x09%s' \"${_id_range[@]}\")\"; then\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:commit-id]: the commits pushed to '$branch_name' could not be read; git's error is above.\" >&2\n        REJECTED=1\n        continue\n      fi\n      _id_missing=\"$(printf '%s\\n' \"$_id_log\" \\\n        | awk -F'\\t' 'NF && ($2 !~ /^[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]$/ || $2 == \"0000000\") { print \"   \" $1 \" \" $3 }')\"\n      if [[ -n \"$_id_missing\" ]]; then\n        {\n          echo \"BLOCKED [gate:commit-id]: commits pushed to '$branch_name' carry no Commit-Id trailer:\"\n          printf '%s\\n' \"$_id_missing\" | head -n 5\n          echo \"   ($(printf '%s\\n' \"$_id_missing\" | wc -l | tr -d ' ') in all)\"\n          echo \"   Stamp them by re-committing each, which runs the stamper:\"\n          echo \"     git rebase -x 'git commit --amend --no-edit' <the commit before the first one>\"\n          echo \"   To exempt this repository: git config --local skilled.commitId false\"\n        } >&2\n        REJECTED=1\n        continue\n      fi\n    fi\n  fi\n\n  # Release branches are the one namespace the naming/permission gates never touch.\n  case \"$branch_name\" in\n    skilled/v*) continue ;;\n  esac\n\n  # Naming gate removed: it never rejected a push the permission gate below would\n  # have allowed, and that gate is what asks a human before a new branch reaches\n  # origin. A wrapper ref is caught there too, as a new branch.\n\n  # ── Gate 2: remote-push permission (every push, new or update) ──\n  if [[ \"$NAMING_AVAILABLE\" -eq 0 ]]; then\n    # Approval keeps its usual meaning: `1` passes an update, the branch name passes\n    # any push, and the live autosync branch keeps its exception.\n    if [[ \"$NAMING_MISSING\" -eq 1 && \"$is_autosync_live\" -eq 0 \\\n          && \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH:-0}\" != \"$branch_name\" ]] \\\n       && ! [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 && \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH:-0}\" = \"1\" ]]; then\n      {\n        echo \"\"\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:remote-permission]: the permission script is missing: $NAMING\"\n        echo \"Without it the remote allowlist cannot be read, so this push needs explicit approval:\"\n        echo \"  SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=1 git push ...   (an update)\"\n        echo \"  SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=$branch_name git push ...   (creating the branch)\"\n        echo \"\"\n      } >&2\n      REJECTED=1\n    fi\n    continue\n  fi\n  # Creating a remote branch is the act that outlives the session: an update can\n  # be reset, but a branch nobody meant to publish stays until someone notices.\n  # The environment bypass authorises updates only — a new remote branch needs\n  # the allowlist, which is a file a person edits.\n  if [[ \"$remote_sha\" =~ ^0+$ ]] \\\n     && ! is_remote_push_allowlisted \"$branch_name\" \\\n     && [[ \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH:-0}\" != \"$branch_name\" ]]; then\n    {\n      echo \"\"\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:remote-create]: '$branch_name' does not exist on origin, and creating it is not something a session may decide.\"\n      echo \"Branches stay local unless a person approves publishing them.\"\n      echo \"To approve permanently, add the branch to:\"\n      echo \"  .opencode/skills/sk-git/scripts/remote-branch-allowlist.txt\"\n      echo \"For a one-off, name the branch rather than using the blanket switch:\"\n      echo \"  SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=$branch_name git push ...\"\n      echo \"A bare SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=1 authorises updates, never creation.\"\n      echo \"\"\n    } >&2\n    REJECTED=1\n    continue\n  fi\n  # `1` approves this push; naming the branch approves it and permits creating\n  # it. Both are per-push acts by whoever runs the command.\n  if [[ \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH:-0}\" = \"1\" || \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH:-0}\" = \"$branch_name\" ]]; then\n    continue\n  fi\n  if is_remote_push_allowlisted \"$branch_name\"; then\n    continue\n  fi\n  # Continuous-integration autosync exception, scoped to the exact live\n  # branch: git-sync.sh's own contract never asks the caller mid-hook (see\n  # references/continuous-integration.md), and the live branch was already an\n  # explicit operator choice (the primary checkout's own branch) before any\n  # session started publishing to it. Any OTHER branch still asks, even with\n  # SPECKIT_AUTOSYNC=1 set — this is not a blanket autosync bypass.\n  if [[ \"$is_autosync_live\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n    continue\n  fi\n  {\n    echo \"\"\n    echo \"BLOCKED [gate:remote-permission]: push to '$branch_name' on origin needs explicit permission for this push.\"\n    echo \"This repo asks before every push to a branch outside the remote allowlist\"\n    echo \"(built in: main, skilled/v*; extend via\"\n    echo \"  .opencode/skills/sk-git/scripts/remote-branch-allowlist.txt).\"\n    echo \"If the operator has approved THIS push, retry with:\"\n    echo \"  SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=1 git push ...\"\n    echo \"\"\n  } >&2\n  REJECTED=1\ndone\n\nif [[ \"$REJECTED\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n  exit 1\nfi\n\n# ── Skill-metadata gate (independently bypassable) ───────────────────────────\n#\n# Runs the fleet-wide skill-root metadata class gate before skill changes reach\n# origin. It exists because both live regressions to date were pushes that grew\n# a skill's doc corpus without regenerating its generated files — CI on the\n# release line only catches that after the fact, while this catches it before.\n# The gate is dependency-free and finishes in about a second. It warns and never\n# blocks: CI enforces the same check on main and skilled/v*, and the hook cannot\n# regenerate bytes for a commit that already exists. A stale result is fixed with\n# `ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs --fix`.\n#\n# The gate reads the working tree, which on a normal push from a clean worktree\n# is exactly what is being pushed. A deliberately partial push from a dirty\n# tree can skip with SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_SKILL_GATE=1.\nif [[ \"${SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_SKILL_GATE:-0}\" != \"1\" ]]; then\n  SKILL_GATE=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs\"\n  # Per-ref ranges avoid coupling a live-branch publish to the wrapper branch's\n  # upstream configuration. New remote branches still defer to CI because they\n  # have no prior remote tree to compare without scanning an unrelated history.\n  if [[ \"$SKILL_CHANGES_PUSHED\" -eq 1 && ! -f \"$SKILL_GATE\" ]]; then\n    # Only a checkout that ships the toolchain has a checker to miss.\n    if _in_toolchain_repo; then\n      echo \"WARNING [gate:skill-root-metadata]: checker is missing: $SKILL_GATE; push gate failed open.\" >&2\n    fi\n  elif [[ -f \"$SKILL_GATE\" && \"$SKILL_CHANGES_PUSHED\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n    if ! node \"$SKILL_GATE\" >&2; then\n      {\n        echo \"\"\n        if [[ \"$AUTOSYNC_LIVE_PUSH\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n          echo \"WARNING [gate:skill-root-metadata]: this live-branch publish carries stale skill metadata.\"\n          echo \"Not blocking: the hook cannot regenerate committed bytes, and CI enforces this on main and skilled/v*.\"\n        else\n          echo \"WARNING [gate:skill-root-metadata]: skill-root metadata is stale for skills being pushed.\"\n          echo \"Not blocking: CI enforces this on every push to main and skilled/v*.\"\n        fi\n        echo \"Fix with:  node .opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs --fix\"\n        echo \"\"\n      } >&2\n    fi\n  fi\nfi\n\n# ── Compiled-routing gate (third gate, independently bypassable) ─────────────\n# Editing a hub's routing inputs invalidates its minted manifest, which silently\n# drops that hub to legacy routing. Nothing errors locally and nothing logs, so it\n# was only ever found after the push, as a failed CI mail — this one check produced\n# the large majority of them. It is dependency-free and runs in a fraction of a\n# second, so it belongs here where it can be acted on rather than after the fact.\n#\n# The test suites that used to run here moved to CI. They were report-only, so the\n# forty seconds they cost every push bought nothing that could block, and their\n# output was filtered too narrowly to say what had failed.\n#\n# The routing guard below does block, because the failure it catches is silent\n# everywhere else: a stale pin makes a hub fall back to serving legacy, every\n# recommendation loses its compiled route, and no error is raised anywhere. The\n# continuous integration job that was supposed to catch it could not see a nested\n# packet file for months, so relying on it alone is what let this stand twice.\n# Skip with SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE=1.\nif [[ \"${SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE:-0}\" != \"1\" ]]; then\n  ROUTE_GUARD=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/bin/compiled-route-guard.cjs\"\n  if [[ ! -f \"$ROUTE_GUARD\" ]] && _in_toolchain_repo; then\n    echo \"\" >&2\n    echo \"BLOCKED [gate:compiled-routing]: route guard is missing: $ROUTE_GUARD\" >&2\n    echo \"Override once, deliberately: SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE=1 git push ...\" >&2\n    exit 1\n  fi\n  if [[ -f \"$ROUTE_GUARD\" ]]; then\n    if ! node \"$ROUTE_GUARD\" >&2; then\n      {\n        echo \"\"\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:compiled-routing]: a hub's routing manifest is stale or drifted.\"\n        echo \"A stale pin makes the hub serve legacy, which silently drops the compiled\"\n        echo \"route from every recommendation. Nothing else reports it, and it has twice\"\n        echo \"stood for several commits here, which is why this now blocks rather than warns.\"\n        echo \"Fix: refresh the hub, then resync its authored copy in the same change —\"\n        echo \"     refreshing alone leaves the runtime and authored manifests disagreeing.\"\n        echo \"     Then rebuild and re-pin that hub's canary before pushing.\"\n        echo \"Override once, deliberately: SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE=1 git push ...\"\n        echo \"\"\n      } >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n  fi\n\n  # The guard above reads the WORKING TREE. That is the tree the author can fix, so\n  # it is the right thing to validate -- but it is not the tree that reaches anyone\n  # else. When a commit carries minted manifests whose source edits are still\n  # uncommitted, the working tree is coherent and the published commit is not: a\n  # fresh clone recomputes different hashes and those hubs fall back to legacy.\n  # That has happened here, and neither this gate nor the commit-time one saw it,\n  # because the commit gate keys on staged inputs and manifests alone do not trigger\n  # it. So assert the pushed commit carries the same routing bytes the guard just\n  # approved. Anything git cannot resolve is skipped rather than blocked: a hook\n  # that guesses is worse than one that misses.\n  ROUTING_BYTES=(\n    '.opencode/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/*/manifest.json'\n    '.opencode/skills/*/hub-router.json'\n    '.opencode/skills/*/mode-registry.json'\n    '.opencode/skills/*/leaf-manifest.json'\n    '.opencode/skills/*/ROUTER.md'\n    '.opencode/skills/*/SKILL.md'\n    '.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/*/manifest.json'\n    '.skilled/skills/*/hub-router.json'\n    '.skilled/skills/*/mode-registry.json'\n    '.skilled/skills/*/leaf-manifest.json'\n    '.skilled/skills/*/ROUTER.md'\n    '.skilled/skills/*/SKILL.md'\n  )\n  # The comparison checks the guard's own verdict, so it runs only where the guard ran,\n  # and only for the pushed commit the working tree belongs to: any other commit would be\n  # compared with a tree it never had.\n  HEAD_SHA=\"$(git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" rev-parse -q --verify HEAD 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n  for pushed_sha in $PUSHED_SHAS; do\n    [[ -f \"$ROUTE_GUARD\" && -n \"$HEAD_SHA\" && \"$pushed_sha\" == \"$HEAD_SHA\" ]] || continue\n    git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" cat-file -e \"${pushed_sha}^{commit}\" 2>/dev/null || continue\n    if ! git_bounded -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" diff --quiet \"$pushed_sha\" -- \"${ROUTING_BYTES[@]}\" 2>/dev/null; then\n      {\n        echo \"\"\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:routing-commit-parity]: the routing bytes you are pushing are\"\n        echo \"not the ones just validated. The guard passed against your working tree, and\"\n        echo \"commit ${pushed_sha:0:10} differs from it in a routing input or a minted manifest.\"\n        echo \"A clone of that commit recomputes different policy hashes and serves legacy.\"\n        echo \"Fix: commit the routing sources and manifests together, then push again.\"\n        echo \"See what differs:\"\n        echo \"  git diff ${pushed_sha:0:10} -- '.skilled/skills/*/hub-router.json' \\\\\"\n        echo \"      '.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/*/manifest.json'\"\n        echo \"Override once, deliberately: SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE=1 git push ...\"\n        echo \"\"\n      } >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n  done\nfi\n\n# ── Track-root gate (independently bypassable) ──────────────────────────────\n# A track root lists its packets in children_ids, and no packet rule ever reads\n# that list, so it drifted in most tracks with nothing reporting it. create.sh\n# --track keeps the list current for each new packet; this blocks a commit that\n# would publish a list and a set of packets that disagree. It reads each pushed\n# commit, not the working tree, because a shared checkout carries other sessions'\n# unfinished packets. A symlinked track holds another repository's files, so the\n# sweep leaves it out. The sweep is dependency-free and runs in well under a second.\n# Skip with SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_TRACK_GATE=1.\nif [[ \"${SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_TRACK_GATE:-0}\" != \"1\" ]] && _in_toolchain_repo && [[ -d \"$REPO_ROOT/specs\" ]]; then\n  TRACK_SWEEP=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/sweep-track-roots.mjs\"\n  if [[ ! -f \"$TRACK_SWEEP\" ]]; then\n    echo \"\" >&2\n    echo \"BLOCKED [gate:track-roots]: sweep is missing: $TRACK_SWEEP\" >&2\n    echo \"Override once, deliberately: SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_TRACK_GATE=1 git push ...\" >&2\n    exit 1\n  else\n    for pushed_sha in $PUSHED_SHAS; do\n      git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" cat-file -e \"${pushed_sha}^{commit}\" 2>/dev/null || continue\n      _track_rc=0\n      _track_out=\"$(node \"$TRACK_SWEEP\" --specs \"$REPO_ROOT/specs\" --rev \"$pushed_sha\" 2>&1)\" || _track_rc=$?\n      if [[ \"$_track_rc\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n        {\n          echo \"\"\n          echo \"BLOCKED [gate:track-roots]: in commit ${pushed_sha:0:10}, a track root's children_ids\"\n          echo \"do not list exactly the packets that track holds:\"\n          echo \"$_track_out\" | sed 's/^/  /'\n          echo \"Fix: node $(dirname \"${TRACK_SWEEP#\"$REPO_ROOT\"/}\")/refresh-track-roots.mjs --apply\"\n          echo \"     then commit each changed graph-metadata.json together with the packets it\"\n          echo \"     lists, and push again.\"\n          echo \"Override once, deliberately: SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_TRACK_GATE=1 git push ...\"\n          echo \"\"\n        } >&2\n        exit 1\n      elif [[ \"$_track_rc\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n        echo \"WARNING [gate:track-roots]: could not sweep commit ${pushed_sha:0:10} (exit $_track_rc); push gate failed open.\" >&2\n        echo \"$_track_out\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n      fi\n    done\n  fi\nfi\n\nexit 0\n\n\n[showing lines 719-1680 of 1680; full output saved to /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/opencode/shell/4c10847297631c3af43d766cf1908920fe00c449/sh_1ceaccd46001kS7dOHs1GL6EYl.out]","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":true,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n  # takes the same layout-relative suffix. The packet's own path comes from the layout\n  # module, so a hook installed machine-wide names no spec packet itself.\n  if ! ROUTE_AUTHORED_DIR=\"$(node -e '\n      process.stdout.write(require(process.argv[1]).AUTHORED_PROGRAM_DIR);\n    ' \"$ROUTE_LAYOUT\" 2>&1)\" || [[ -z \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_DIR\" ]]; then\n    echo \"\" >&2\n    echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: could not read the authored program dir from compiled-route-layout.cjs:\" >&2\n    printf '%s\\n' \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_DIR\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n    echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n    exit 1\n  fi\n  ROUTE_AUTHORED=\"$REPO_ROOT/$ROUTE_AUTHORED_DIR/${ROUTE_RUNTIME#\"$ROUTE_SOURCE/bin/lib/compiled-routing/\"}\"\n  for ROUTE_HUB in $ROUTE_HUBS; do\n    # mode-registry.json and hub-router.json are read directly by the hash. Every\n    # SKILL.md under the hub reaches it through the leaves the registry declares, and\n    # so does the ROUTER.md beside each one, which holds that skill's machine router.\n    # A git pathspec glob crosses \"/\", so the last pattern matches a SKILL.md at any\n    # depth. That is wider than the two levels that exist today and is the safe\n    # direction: a hub that grows a deeper mode keeps its manifest minted.\n    # --diff-filter=ACMR excludes a staged deletion, which would otherwise mint from\n    # a tree the leaf has already left and block with a message about the wrong thing.\n    # This set is narrower than the trigger paths in routing-registry-drift.yml on\n    # purpose. That workflow decides whether running the check is worthwhile and can\n    # afford a false positive; this decides what to rewrite and stage, where a false\n    # positive would re-mint on an edit that changed nothing.\n    ROUTE_PATHS=(\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/SKILL.md\"\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/hub-router.json\"\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/mode-registry.json\"\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/*/SKILL.md\"\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/ROUTER.md\"\n      \".opencode/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/*/ROUTER.md\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/SKILL.md\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/hub-router.json\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/mode-registry.json\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/*/SKILL.md\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/ROUTER.md\"\n      \".skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB/*/ROUTER.md\"\n    )\n    ROUTE_STAGED=\"$(git_bounded diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR -- \"${ROUTE_PATHS[@]}\" 2>/dev/null)\"\n    [[ -z \"$ROUTE_STAGED\" ]] && continue\n\n    # git runs this hook against a throwaway index when a pathspec narrows the commit\n    # (\"git commit -- path\"), named next-index-<pid>.lock. Anything staged there is\n    # discarded, so the manifest would reach the commit while the real index keeps the\n    # old blob and the next commit silently reverts it. Auto-fixing is unsafe in that\n    # mode, so say so and let the author run the mint themselves.\n    # The name is the whole discriminator, and it has to be: a plain commit runs with\n    # .git/index and \"git commit -a\" with .git/index.lock, which IS renamed into place,\n    # so testing for \"not index\" would block every -a commit in the repository.\n    if [[ \"$(basename \"${GIT_INDEX_FILE:-index}\")\" == next-index-* ]]; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: $ROUTE_HUB needs a re-mint, and this commit narrows its\" >&2\n      echo \"pathspec, so anything staged now would be discarded with the temporary index.\" >&2\n      echo \"Fix: node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-manifest.cjs refresh --hub $ROUTE_HUB \\\\\" >&2\n      echo \"       --skill-root .skilled/skills/$ROUTE_HUB\" >&2\n      echo \"     then copy the runtime manifest over its authored copy, and commit both.\" >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n\n    # Minting reads the working tree, not the index. If an input is only half staged\n    # the manifest would describe content this commit does not carry, which is a\n    # quieter version of the drift this gate exists to remove. Refuse instead.\n    ROUTE_DIRTY=\"$(git_bounded diff --name-only -- \"${ROUTE_PATHS[@]}\" 2>/dev/null)\"\n    if [[ -n \"$ROUTE_DIRTY\" ]]; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: $ROUTE_HUB has routing inputs staged and unstaged at once:\" >&2\n      printf '%s\\n' \"$ROUTE_DIRTY\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n      echo \"A manifest minted now would describe content this commit does not contain.\" >&2\n      echo \"Fix: stage the rest, or unstage the partial edit, then re-commit.\" >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n\n    ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE=\"$ROUTE_RUNTIME/$ROUTE_HUB/manifest.json\"\n    ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE=\"$ROUTE_AUTHORED/$ROUTE_HUB/manifest.json\"\n    # Fail closed on every precondition. The old shape guarded the copy, the add and\n    # the report behind one existence test, so a missing authored manifest skipped all\n    # three and exited zero with nothing staged and nothing printed.\n    for ROUTE_NEEDED in \"$ROUTE_MINT\" \"$ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE\" \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\"; do\n      if [[ ! -f \"$ROUTE_NEEDED\" ]]; then\n        echo \"\" >&2\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: $ROUTE_HUB cannot be re-minted, missing:\" >&2\n        echo \"  $ROUTE_NEEDED\" >&2\n        echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n        exit 1\n      fi\n    done\n\n    # Captured rather than re-run on failure: refresh writes before it reports, so a\n    # second invocation to show the operator its output would write a second time.\n    if ! ROUTE_OUT=\"$(node \"$ROUTE_MINT\" refresh --hub \"$ROUTE_HUB\" \\\n         --skill-root \"$ROUTE_SOURCE/skills/$ROUTE_HUB\" 2>&1)\"; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: re-minting $ROUTE_HUB failed.\" >&2\n      printf '%s\\n' \"$ROUTE_OUT\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n\n    # The runtime manifest is what serves. The authored copy is what a rebuild would\n    # reinstate, so leaving it behind reintroduces the same staleness on the next one.\n    if ! cp \"$ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE\" \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\" 2>/dev/null; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: could not copy the $ROUTE_HUB manifest to its authored copy:\" >&2\n      echo \"  $ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\" >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n    git_bounded add -- \"$ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE\" \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\"\n\n    # Prove it. An add that silently did nothing leaves the commit carrying a stale\n    # manifest, and no later local gate can see that, so confirm both paths are in\n    # the index carrying exactly what the mint just wrote.\n    #\n    # Ask that question directly, per path: tracked, and no worktree-versus-index\n    # difference. The earlier form counted paths whose staged blob differed from\n    # HEAD, which is a different proposition and wrong in both directions. A mint\n    # that produced no change left both manifests equal to HEAD, so the count was\n    # zero and a correct commit was blocked. Worse, a stale blob already staged from\n    # an earlier run still differed from HEAD, so the count was two and a failed add\n    # passed, which is precisely the case this check exists to catch.\n    ROUTE_CONFIRM=0\n    for ROUTE_MANIFEST in \"$ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE\" \"$ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\"; do\n      if git_bounded ls-files --error-unmatch -- \"$ROUTE_MANIFEST\" >/dev/null 2>&1 \\\n         && git_bounded diff --quiet -- \"$ROUTE_MANIFEST\" 2>/dev/null; then\n        ROUTE_CONFIRM=$((ROUTE_CONFIRM + 1))\n      fi\n    done\n    if [[ \"$ROUTE_CONFIRM\" != \"2\" ]]; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:route-remint]: re-minted $ROUTE_HUB but only $ROUTE_CONFIRM of 2 manifests reached the index.\" >&2\n      echo \"Fix: git add the two manifest paths below, then re-commit.\" >&2\n      echo \"  $ROUTE_RUNTIME_FILE\" >&2\n      echo \"  $ROUTE_AUTHORED_FILE\" >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_ROUTE_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n    echo \"[gate:route-remint] re-minted $ROUTE_HUB and staged both manifests.\" >&2\n  done\n  fi\nfi\n\n# --- Deep command contract auto recompile (blocking only when it cannot fix it) ---\n# Each compiled deep command contract records a digest of every source it was built\n# from: the deep-loop skill docs and the deep command assets. The doc-version gate\n# above rewrites those docs, so a contract compiled before the commit is stale by the\n# time the commit lands, and CI's contract drift check then fails. Recompile only when\n# a deep-loop source is staged and the drift check reports a difference.\n# Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_CONTRACT_RECOMPILE=1 git commit ...\nif [[ \"${SPECKIT_SKIP_CONTRACT_RECOMPILE:-0}\" != \"1\" ]] && command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  CONTRACT_COMPILER=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/compile-command-contracts.cjs\"\n  CONTRACT_CHECK=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/check-contract-drift.cjs\"\n  CONTRACT_REL=\"${SOURCE_ROOT#\"$REPO_ROOT\"/}\"\n  if [[ -f \"$CONTRACT_COMPILER\" && -f \"$CONTRACT_CHECK\" ]] \\\n     && [[ -n \"$(git_bounded diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR -- \\\n          \"$CONTRACT_REL/skills/system-deep-loop/\" \"$CONTRACT_REL/commands/deep/\" 2>/dev/null)\" ]] \\\n     && ! node \"$CONTRACT_CHECK\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    # The compiler owns the command list, so a fourth deep command needs no hook edit.\n    if ! CONTRACT_COMMANDS=\"$(node -e 'console.log(Object.keys(require(process.argv[1]).COMMANDS).join(\"\\n\"))' \\\n         \"$CONTRACT_COMPILER\" 2>&1)\"; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:contract-recompile]: could not read the command list from compile-command-contracts.cjs:\" >&2\n      printf '%s\\n' \"$CONTRACT_COMMANDS\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_CONTRACT_RECOMPILE=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n    for CONTRACT_COMMAND in $CONTRACT_COMMANDS; do\n      # The compiler prints each file it wrote, one path per line, so those are what\n      # gets staged.\n      if ! CONTRACT_OUT=\"$(node \"$CONTRACT_COMPILER\" --command \"$CONTRACT_COMMAND\" --write 2>&1)\"; then\n        echo \"\" >&2\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:contract-recompile]: compiling $CONTRACT_COMMAND failed.\" >&2\n        printf '%s\\n' \"$CONTRACT_OUT\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n        echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_CONTRACT_RECOMPILE=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n        exit 1\n      fi\n      while IFS= read -r CONTRACT_FILE; do\n        [[ -n \"$CONTRACT_FILE\" && -f \"$REPO_ROOT/$CONTRACT_FILE\" ]] && git_bounded add -- \"$REPO_ROOT/$CONTRACT_FILE\"\n      done <<< \"$CONTRACT_OUT\"\n    done\n    if ! CONTRACT_AFTER=\"$(node \"$CONTRACT_CHECK\" 2>&1)\"; then\n      echo \"\" >&2\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:contract-recompile]: the deep command contracts still drift after a recompile:\" >&2\n      printf '%s\\n' \"$CONTRACT_AFTER\" | tail -6 | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n      echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_CONTRACT_RECOMPILE=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n    echo \"[gate:contract-recompile] recompiled the deep command contracts and staged them.\" >&2\n  fi\nfi\n\n# --- Spec derived-metadata auto re-mint (blocking only when it cannot fix it) ---\n# A packet's graph-metadata.json stores a fingerprint of the spec documents it was\n# derived from, plus fields summarised out of them. Editing spec.md or any sibling\n# doc invalidates both, and nothing local says so: validate.sh reports it, but only\n# for the folder someone thinks to run it against. A repository sweep found 460\n# packets carrying a stale fingerprint, which is what happens when every edit leaves\n# the regeneration to a human who has no reason to remember it.\n# Same shape as the routing gate above and for the same reason: the artifact is\n# derived from the staged documents, so this regenerates and stages it rather than\n# printing a command. It refuses in the two cases where regenerating would be a lie.\nif [[ \"${SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT:-0}\" != \"1\" ]] && command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  # scratch/ holds working files that are not packet documents, and z_archive/ is\n  # retired history nothing should re-derive.\n  SPEC_STAGED_DOCS=\"$(git_bounded diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR -- 'specs/**/*.md' 2>/dev/null \\\n    | grep -v '/scratch/' | grep -v '/z_archive/' || true)\"\n\n  if [[ -n \"$SPEC_STAGED_DOCS\" ]]; then\n    # A doc's packet is the nearest ancestor carrying BOTH graph-metadata.json and\n    # spec.md. Walking up rather than assuming a depth is what makes this work for a\n    # phase child, whose docs sit two levels below the track.\n    # Both files are required because the repository contains grouping directories\n    # that carry metadata without being packets, and the re-derive tool exits non-zero\n    # on them (\"target is not a spec folder\"). Keying on the metadata alone would make\n    # this gate block every commit that touched a document under one of those.\n    SPEC_PACKETS=\"$(\n      while IFS= read -r doc; do\n        [[ -z \"$doc\" ]] && continue\n        dir=\"$(dirname \"$doc\")\"\n        while [[ \"$dir\" == specs/* ]]; do\n          if [[ -f \"$REPO_ROOT/$dir/graph-metadata.json\" && -f \"$REPO_ROOT/$dir/spec.md\" ]]; then\n            echo \"$dir\"; break\n          fi\n          dir=\"$(dirname \"$dir\")\"\n        done\n      done <<< \"$SPEC_STAGED_DOCS\" | sort -u\n    )\"\n\n    # A repository that does not ship the toolchain has no re-derive tool and nothing\n    # to re-derive with, so it passes. Inside one that ships it, a missing tool blocks.\n    SPEC_TOOL=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/repair-derived.cjs\"\n    if [[ -n \"$SPEC_PACKETS\" ]] && { [[ -f \"$SPEC_TOOL\" ]] || _in_toolchain_repo; }; then\n      # git runs this hook against a throwaway index when a pathspec narrows the\n      # commit. Anything staged there is discarded, so the metadata would reach the\n      # commit while the real index keeps the old blob, and the next commit would\n      # silently revert it. The name is the whole discriminator: a plain commit uses\n      # .git/index and \"git commit -a\" uses .git/index.lock, so testing for \"not\n      # index\" would block every -a commit in the repository.\n      if [[ \"$(basename \"${GIT_INDEX_FILE:-index}\")\" == next-index-* ]]; then\n        echo \"\" >&2\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:spec-remint]: spec docs are staged and this commit narrows its\" >&2\n        echo \"pathspec, so metadata staged now would be discarded with the temporary index.\" >&2\n        echo \"Fix: run the repair yourself and commit its output with the docs:\" >&2\n        printf '  node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/repair-derived.cjs --folder %s --apply\\n' $SPEC_PACKETS >&2\n        echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n        exit 1\n      fi\n\n      # Partial staging is checked per packet and up front, because a refusal has\n      # to name the packet and must happen before anything is written.\n      # Re-deriving reads the working tree, not the index. If a doc in a packet is\n      # only half staged the metadata would describe content the commit does not\n      # carry, which is a quieter version of the drift this gate exists to remove.\n      SPEC_ARGS=()\n      # Bash 3.2 ships on macOS and errors on an empty array expansion under\n      # set -u. The guard above guarantees at least one packet, so this is belt\n      # and braces rather than a live path, and it costs nothing.\n      for SPEC_PACKET in $SPEC_PACKETS; do\n        SPEC_DIRTY=\"$(git_bounded diff --name-only -- \"$SPEC_PACKET\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v '/scratch/' || true)\"\n        # Only a half-staged INPUT is a problem: re-deriving reads the working tree,\n        # so the gate's own derived outputs (graph-metadata.json, description.json)\n        # are outputs, not inputs, and a packet dirty only in those re-derives\n        # cleanly. Block only when a real document is half-staged.\n        SPEC_DIRTY_INPUTS=\"\"\n        if [[ -n \"$SPEC_DIRTY\" ]]; then\n          SPEC_DIRTY_INPUTS=\"$(printf '%s\\n' \"$SPEC_DIRTY\" \\\n            | grep -vE '/(graph-metadata|description)\\.json$' || true)\"\n        fi\n        if [[ -n \"$SPEC_DIRTY_INPUTS\" ]]; then\n          echo \"\" >&2\n          echo \"BLOCKED [gate:spec-remint]: $SPEC_PACKET has documents staged and unstaged at once:\" >&2\n          printf '%s\\n' \"$SPEC_DIRTY_INPUTS\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n          echo \"Metadata derived now would describe content this commit does not contain.\" >&2\n          echo \"Fix: stage the rest, or unstage the partial edit, then re-commit.\" >&2\n          echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n          exit 1\n        fi\n        SPEC_ARGS+=(--folder \"$SPEC_PACKET\")\n      done\n\n      # One process for every packet. The re-derive itself is nearly free when a\n      # packet is already fresh, so per-packet spawning was the entire cost: a\n      # no-op packet measures about 1.5s wall against 0.7s of work, and a commit\n      # touching scores of packets paid that startup once each.\n      if [[ ! -f \"$SPEC_TOOL\" ]]; then\n        echo \"\" >&2\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:spec-remint]: re-derive tool is missing: $SPEC_TOOL\" >&2\n        echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n        exit 1\n      fi\n      SPEC_RC=0\n      SPEC_OUT=\"$(node \"$SPEC_TOOL\" \\\n            ${SPEC_ARGS[@]+\"${SPEC_ARGS[@]}\"} --apply 2>&1)\" || SPEC_RC=$?\n\n      # One call now covers every packet, so a failure can leave earlier packets\n      # already rewritten on disk. Leaving those unstaged makes the next attempt\n      # refuse a packet that is not the problem, reporting a half-staged tree\n      # instead of the real failure. Stage what succeeded either way, then decide.\n      # Which packets the tool actually rewrote has to be decided BEFORE staging,\n      # because staging is what clears the unstaged-diff signal that answers it.\n      SPEC_WROTE=()\n      for SPEC_PACKET in $SPEC_PACKETS; do\n        SPEC_PAIR=(\"$SPEC_PACKET/graph-metadata.json\" \"$SPEC_PACKET/description.json\")\n        SPEC_CHANGED=\"$(git_bounded diff --name-only -- \"${SPEC_PAIR[@]}\" 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n        [[ -z \"$SPEC_CHANGED\" ]] && continue\n        SPEC_WROTE+=(\"$SPEC_PACKET\")\n        git_bounded add -- \"${SPEC_PAIR[@]}\" 2>/dev/null || true\n      done\n\n      if [[ \"$SPEC_RC\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n        echo \"\" >&2\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:spec-remint]: could not re-derive metadata for the staged packets:\" >&2\n        printf '%s\\n' \"$SPEC_OUT\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n        echo \"Any packet the tool did repair has been staged, so a retry reports this\" >&2\n        echo \"same failure rather than a half-written tree.\" >&2\n        echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n        exit 1\n      fi\n\n      # Confirmation stays per packet so a path that fails to reach the index is\n      # named. Nothing to stage is the common case: most doc edits leave the\n      # derived fields identical, and the tool is a no-op then, so only the\n      # packets recorded above are confirmed and reported.\n      for SPEC_PACKET in ${SPEC_WROTE[@]+\"${SPEC_WROTE[@]}\"}; do\n        SPEC_PAIR=(\"$SPEC_PACKET/graph-metadata.json\" \"$SPEC_PACKET/description.json\")\n\n        # Prove it. An add that silently did nothing leaves the commit carrying stale\n        # metadata, and no later local gate can see that.\n        SPEC_LEFT=\"$(git_bounded diff --name-only -- \"${SPEC_PAIR[@]}\" 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n        if [[ -n \"$SPEC_LEFT\" ]]; then\n          echo \"\" >&2\n          echo \"BLOCKED [gate:spec-remint]: re-derived $SPEC_PACKET but this did not reach the index:\" >&2\n          printf '%s\\n' \"$SPEC_LEFT\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n          echo \"Fix: git add those paths, then re-commit.\" >&2\n          echo \"Bypass: SPECKIT_SKIP_SPEC_REMINT=1 git commit ...\" >&2\n          exit 1\n        fi\n        echo \"[gate:spec-remint] re-derived $SPEC_PACKET and staged its metadata.\" >&2\n      done\n    fi\n  fi\nfi\n\n# --- Tool ownership map gate (blocking) ---\n# Blocks commits when the committed generated map no longer matches TOOL_DEFINITIONS.\n# Manual-testing-playbook operator contract: enforced by CI only (pull request,\n# push to main, and a nightly cron), which is broader than this copy could be.\n\nexit 0\n=== pre-push\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Pre-push hook — independent safety and consistency gates run before a push:\n#\n#   Mass deletion:     blocks a push that deletes more tracked files than\n#                      SPECKIT_MASS_DELETION_THRESHOLD (default 100);\n#                      SPECKIT_ALLOW_MASS_DELETION=1 authorizes one push.\n#   Permission:        ANY push (new branch OR update) to a branch outside\n#                      the remote allowlist needs an explicit ask-first\n#                      confirmation for THIS push specifically — a prior\n#                      approval for an earlier push does not carry forward.\n#                      Release branches (skilled/v*) are exempt, and the\n#                      live-branch autosync publish (SPECKIT_AUTOSYNC=1 with\n#                      SPECKIT_LIVE_BRANCH=<branch>) passes for updates to\n#                      that one branch.\n#   Skill metadata:    warns when skill changes leave root metadata stale;\n#                      never blocks (SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_SKILL_GATE=1 skips).\n#   Compiled routing:  the route guard must pass, and a pushed commit that is\n#                      HEAD must carry the routing bytes the guard approved\n#                      (SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE=1 skips both).\n#   Track roots:       each pushed ref's tip must have track roots that list\n#                      exactly the packets they hold\n#                      (SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_TRACK_GATE=1 skips).\n#   Message contract:  every commit the push adds and every new branch name\n#                      must meet the rules the repository's own templates\n#                      declare. This is what catches a commit made with\n#                      --no-verify, and it has no bypass.\n#   Commit-Id:         in every repository lib/commit-id-policy.sh covers,\n#                      each non-merge commit the push adds must carry a\n#                      Commit-Id; no bypass, but git config --local\n#                      skilled.commitId false exempts the repository.\n#\n# The gates are independently bypassable; skipping one never skips another.\n# The hooks are installed machine-wide. Scripts from a repository's own tree run\n# only in the checkout the hooks live in (or one of its worktrees), or where the\n# repository's local config sets skilled.trustRepoHooks=true.\n#\n# Protocol: git feeds \"<local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>\"\n# lines on stdin, one per ref being pushed. A remote sha of all-zeros means\n# the push CREATES a new remote branch.\n#\n# Bypass permission only: SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=1 git push ...\n# Allowlist file:         .skilled/skills/sk-git/scripts/remote-branch-allowlist.txt\n# Install:   bash .skilled/scripts/install-git-hooks.sh\n# Uninstall: bash .skilled/scripts/install-git-hooks.sh --uninstall\n\nset -euo pipefail\n\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || true)\"\nif [[ -z \"$REPO_ROOT\" ]]; then\n  exit 0\nfi\n\n# >>> source-root selection (identical in every hook script)\n# The source tree sits under .skilled or .opencode, and a checkout may carry both, one,\n# or an empty placeholder of either. The authored sentinel inside the tree decides,\n# never a directory existing, and .skilled wins when both carry it. These scripts run\n# for every repository on the machine, so a missing file means either this repository\n# ships no toolchain or its install is broken, and the same sentinel tells them apart.\nSOURCE_ROOT=\"$REPO_ROOT/.skilled\"\n[[ -f \"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md\" ]] || SOURCE_ROOT=\"$REPO_ROOT/.opencode\"\n_in_toolchain_repo() {\n  [[ -n \"$REPO_ROOT\" && -f \"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md\" ]]\n}\n# <<< source-root selection\n# >>> hook trust (identical in every global hook script)\n# These scripts are installed once for every repository on the machine, and the block\n# above picks the source tree from the repository the hook fires in. A cloned repository\n# controls that tree, so its scripts run only when it shares a git common dir with the\n# checkout these hooks live in (that checkout or one of its worktrees), or when its local\n# config sets skilled.trustRepoHooks=true. Local config is never cloned, and command-line\n# or environment config does not count, so a repository cannot opt itself in. Trust\n# covers the repository, not a branch: any branch checked out in a trusted checkout or\n# its worktrees runs its own tree's scripts, so review an untrusted branch in a separate\n# clone. An untrusted repository is treated as one without the toolchain.\n_trust_self=\"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\"\nwhile [[ -L \"$_trust_self\" ]]; do\n  _trust_link=\"$(readlink \"$_trust_self\")\"\n  case \"$_trust_link\" in /*) _trust_self=\"$_trust_link\" ;; *) _trust_self=\"$(dirname \"$_trust_self\")/$_trust_link\" ;; esac\ndone\n_hook_common=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$_trust_self\")\" 2>/dev/null && env -u GIT_DIR -u GIT_WORK_TREE -u GIT_COMMON_DIR -u GIT_INDEX_FILE git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n_repo_common=\"$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null || true)\"\nif [[ -n \"$REPO_ROOT\" && ( -z \"$_hook_common\" || \"$_hook_common\" != \"$_repo_common\" ) ]] \\\n  && [[ \"$(git config --local --bool skilled.trustRepoHooks 2>/dev/null || true)\" != \"true\" ]]; then\n  # /dev/null is never a directory, so no repository can ship files under this root.\n  SOURCE_ROOT=\"/dev/null/.skilled-untrusted\"\nfi\n# <<< hook trust\n\n# Every git call below that reads the index or the working tree goes through\n# git_bounded: fsmonitor off and a wall-clock bound, so a wedged daemon cannot hang a\n# push. A timeout is recorded and the exit trap turns it into a refusal, whatever the\n# gate that made the call would have decided with an empty answer. Without the helper\n# the calls run unbounded, as before, with fsmonitor still off.\n_bounded_git_lib=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$_trust_self\")\" && pwd -P)/lib/bounded-git.sh\"\nif [[ -r \"$_bounded_git_lib\" ]]; then\n  # shellcheck source=lib/bounded-git.sh\n  . \"$_bounded_git_lib\"\n  git_bound_arm\nelse\n  echo \"WARNING [gate:git-timeout]: $_bounded_git_lib is missing, so git calls here run without a time bound.\" >&2\n  git_bounded() { git -c core.fsmonitor=false \"$@\"; }\n  git_bound_finish() { exit \"$1\"; }\nfi\n_pre_push_exit() {\n  local rc=$?\n  git_bound_finish \"$rc\"\n}\ntrap _pre_push_exit EXIT\n\n# Mass-deletion guard: source if present. The gate inside the loop checks that\n# its function actually loaded before using it, so a lib that fails to load means\n# the deletion ceiling is not enforced (fail-open). A lib missing from a checkout\n# that ships the toolchain is a broken install instead, and the gate then blocks\n# each update push until SPECKIT_ALLOW_MASS_DELETION=1 approves it.\n_MASS_DEL_GUARD=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/scripts/git-hooks/lib/mass-deletion-guard.sh\"\n_MASS_DEL_MISSING=0\nif [[ -f \"$_MASS_DEL_GUARD\" ]]; then\n  set +e\n  # shellcheck source=/dev/null\n  . \"$_MASS_DEL_GUARD\" 2>/dev/null\n  _mass_guard_rc=$?\n  set -e\n  if [[ \"$_mass_guard_rc\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n    echo \"WARNING [gate:mass-deletion]: guard unavailable; push gate failed open.\" >&2\n  fi\nelif _in_toolchain_repo; then\n  _MASS_DEL_MISSING=1\nfi\n\n# --- Validator load: a broken validator never blocks a push, and a missing one ---\n# --- blocks only in a checkout that ships the toolchain ---\nNAMING=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/sk-git/scripts/worktree-naming.sh\"\nNAMING_AVAILABLE=1\nNAMING_MISSING=0\nif [[ ! -f \"$NAMING\" ]]; then\n  # Without the script the remote allowlist cannot be read, so the permission gate\n  # below asks for approval rather than disappearing. A repository without the\n  # toolchain has no allowlist to read and gets no new output from these hooks.\n  if _in_toolchain_repo; then\n    NAMING_MISSING=1\n  fi\n  NAMING_AVAILABLE=0\nelse\n  # A broken validator must not suppress the independent metadata and test gates.\n  set +e\n  # shellcheck source=/dev/null\n  source \"$NAMING\" 2>/dev/null\n  _src_rc=$?\n  set -e\n  if [[ \"$_src_rc\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n    echo \"⚠️  pre-push: failed to source worktree-naming.sh — skipping the remote-push-permission gate.\" >&2\n    NAMING_AVAILABLE=0\n  elif ! command -v is_remote_push_allowlisted >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    echo \"⚠️  pre-push: worktree-naming.sh loaded without is_remote_push_allowlisted — skipping the remote-push-permission gate.\" >&2\n    NAMING_AVAILABLE=0\n  fi\nfi\n\n\n\n# --- Message contract: the rules come from the pushed repository's templates, ---\n# --- the validator from beside this hook ---\nREMOTE_NAME=\"${1:-origin}\"\n_self=\"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\"\nwhile [[ -L \"$_self\" ]]; do\n  _link=\"$(readlink \"$_self\")\"\n  case \"$_link\" in /*) _self=\"$_link\" ;; *) _self=\"$(dirname \"$_self\")/$_link\" ;; esac\ndone\nHOOK_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$_self\")\" && pwd -P)\"\n# Gate settings: a gate the operator switched off in git config (speckit.hooks.<key>)\n# runs as if its SPECKIT_SKIP_* variable were set. Without the helper every gate runs.\n# Other repositories run none of these gates, so they read no setting and print nothing.\nif _in_toolchain_repo && [[ -r \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/gate-config.sh\" ]]; then\n  # shellcheck source=lib/gate-config.sh\n  . \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/gate-config.sh\"\n  gate_config_apply pre-push\nfi\nCONTRACT_GATE_READY=0\nCONTRACT_GATE_BROKEN=\"\"\nif [[ -f \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/message-contract-gate.sh\" ]]; then\n  # shellcheck source=lib/message-contract-gate.sh\n  . \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/message-contract-gate.sh\"\n  CONTRACT_VALIDATOR=\"$(mcg_validator_path \"$HOOK_DIR\" \"$REPO_ROOT\")\"\n  _unavailable=\"$(mcg_unavailable_reason \"$CONTRACT_VALIDATOR\")\"\n  if [[ -z \"$_unavailable\" ]]; then\n    CONTRACT_GATE_READY=1\n  elif mcg_repo_declares_rules \"$REPO_ROOT\" commit-message-template.md \\\n       || mcg_repo_declares_rules \"$REPO_ROOT\" worktree-checklist.md; then\n    CONTRACT_GATE_BROKEN=\"$_unavailable\"\n  fi\nelif _in_toolchain_repo; then\n  CONTRACT_GATE_BROKEN=\"the hook helpers are missing at $HOOK_DIR/lib\"\nfi\n\n# --- Commit-Id presence: the policy comes from beside this hook, never from the ---\n# --- pushed repository, so the trust gate cannot switch it off ---\nCOMMIT_ID_GATE=0\nCOMMIT_ID_BROKEN=0\nif [[ -f \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/commit-id-policy.sh\" ]]; then\n  # shellcheck source=lib/commit-id-policy.sh\n  . \"$HOOK_DIR/lib/commit-id-policy.sh\"\n  if cip_repo_wants_ids \"$REPO_ROOT\"; then\n    COMMIT_ID_GATE=1\n  fi\nelif _in_toolchain_repo; then\n  COMMIT_ID_BROKEN=1\nfi\n\nREJECTED=0\nAUTOSYNC_LIVE_PUSH=0\nSKILL_CHANGES_PUSHED=0\nPUSHED_SHAS=\"\"\n\nwhile read -r _ local_sha remote_ref remote_sha; do\n  [[ -n \"$remote_ref\" ]] || continue\n\n  case \"$remote_ref\" in\n    refs/heads/*) branch_name=\"${remote_ref#refs/heads/}\" ;;\n    *) continue ;;   # tags and other ref types sit outside both gates\n  esac\n\n  is_autosync_live=0\n  if [[ \"${SPECKIT_AUTOSYNC:-0}\" = \"1\" && -n \"${SPECKIT_LIVE_BRANCH:-}\" && \"$branch_name\" = \"$SPECKIT_LIVE_BRANCH\" ]]; then\n    is_autosync_live=1\n    AUTOSYNC_LIVE_PUSH=1\n  fi\n\n  is_new=0\n  if [[ \"$remote_sha\" =~ ^0+$ ]]; then\n    is_new=1\n  fi\n\n  # ── Gate 0: mass-deletion ceiling — runs for EVERY branch, releases included.\n  # The destructive stale-tree push that motivated this guard went to a release\n  # branch, which the naming and permission gates deliberately skip; this gate\n  # must not. Updates only: a brand-new branch has no prior remote state to\n  # delete from. Fail-open if the guard lib did not load.\n  if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 && \"$_MASS_DEL_MISSING\" -eq 1 && \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_MASS_DELETION:-0}\" != \"1\" ]]; then\n    echo \"BLOCKED [gate:mass-deletion]: guard library is missing: $_MASS_DEL_GUARD\" >&2\n    echo \"Approve this push once, deliberately: SPECKIT_ALLOW_MASS_DELETION=1 git push ...\" >&2\n    REJECTED=1\n    continue\n  fi\n  if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 ]] && command -v mass_deletion_verdict >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    _mdg_pushdel=\"$(mass_deletion_range_count \"$remote_sha\" \"$local_sha\")\"\n    if ! mass_deletion_verdict \"$_mdg_pushdel\"; then\n      mass_deletion_report push \"$_mdg_pushdel\" \"($branch_name $remote_sha..$local_sha)\"\n      # An autosync publish to the live branch needs a different explanation:\n      # the operator is not at the keyboard, so the generic stale-tree message\n      # reads as an unexpected hook failure. Name the mass-deletion ceiling as\n      # the cause and point at the manual publish path for the session's work.\n      if [[ \"$is_autosync_live\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n        echo \"AUTOSYNC BLOCKED [gate:mass-deletion]: live branch '$branch_name'.\" >&2\n        echo \"   The mass-deletion ceiling (${SPECKIT_MASS_DELETION_THRESHOLD:-100} tracked files) blocked it.\" >&2\n        echo \"   A session likely committed against a stale tree. Inspect the range, then publish manually.\" >&2\n        echo \"   Manual publish:  bash .skilled/bin/git-sync.sh --live $branch_name\" >&2\n      fi\n      REJECTED=1\n      continue\n    fi\n  fi\n\n  if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 ]] && ! git_bounded -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" diff --quiet \"$remote_sha\" \"$local_sha\" -- .opencode/skills .skilled/skills 2>/dev/null; then\n    SKILL_CHANGES_PUSHED=1\n  fi\n\n  PUSHED_SHAS=\"${PUSHED_SHAS} ${local_sha}\"\n\n  # ── Gate 6: message contract — every branch, releases and main included ──\n  # A deletion pushes no commits. Only what this push adds is checked: commits that\n  # no remote-tracking ref holds, of any remote, since the target may be a URL or a\n  # remote never fetched, and for an update nothing the old remote tip holds. An old\n  # tip missing from the local object store (a force-push over unfetched work) cannot\n  # be excluded, so the remote-tracking refs bound the range alone. The ref being\n  # overwritten is excluded from the Commit-Id check so a rebase is not compared\n  # with itself.\n  if [[ ! \"$local_sha\" =~ ^0+$ ]]; then\n    if [[ -n \"$CONTRACT_GATE_BROKEN\" ]]; then\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:message-contract]: this repository declares message rules, but $CONTRACT_GATE_BROKEN.\" >&2\n      REJECTED=1\n      continue\n    fi\n    if [[ \"$CONTRACT_GATE_READY\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n      _range=\"$local_sha --not --remotes\"\n      if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 ]] && git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" cat-file -e \"${remote_sha}^{commit}\" 2>/dev/null; then\n        _range=\"$local_sha --not $remote_sha --remotes\"\n      fi\n      _contract_rc=0\n      node \"$CONTRACT_VALIDATOR\" --repo \"$REPO_ROOT\" --rev-list \"$_range\" \\\n        --exclude-ref \"refs/remotes/$REMOTE_NAME/$branch_name\" || _contract_rc=$?\n      if [[ \"$_contract_rc\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:message-contract]: commits pushed to '$branch_name' break the commit rules above.\" >&2\n        echo \"   Reword them (git rebase -i, then reword) and push again.\" >&2\n        REJECTED=1\n        continue\n      elif [[ \"$_contract_rc\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:message-contract]: the message validator could not check the commits pushed to '$branch_name' (exit $_contract_rc); its error is above.\" >&2\n        REJECTED=1\n        continue\n      fi\n      if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 1 ]] && ! node \"$CONTRACT_VALIDATOR\" --repo \"$REPO_ROOT\" --branch \"$branch_name\"; then\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:message-contract]: new branch '$branch_name' breaks the naming rules above.\" >&2\n        REJECTED=1\n        continue\n      fi\n    fi\n  fi\n\n  # ── Gate 7: Commit-Id presence, every branch, no bypass ──\n  # Catches what commit-msg cannot: a commit made with --no-verify or by a tool\n  # that runs no hooks. The range is Gate 6's, and merges are exempt as in the\n  # stamper. git config --local skilled.commitId false exempts the repository.\n  if [[ ! \"$local_sha\" =~ ^0+$ ]]; then\n    if [[ \"$COMMIT_ID_BROKEN\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:commit-id]: the hook helpers are missing at $HOOK_DIR/lib; re-run: bash .skilled/scripts/install-git-hooks.sh\" >&2\n      REJECTED=1\n      continue\n    fi\n    # git only offers a local sha it holds, so one that does not resolve has nothing to check.\n    if [[ \"$COMMIT_ID_GATE\" -eq 1 ]] && git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" cat-file -e \"${local_sha}^{commit}\" 2>/dev/null; then\n      _id_range=(\"$local_sha\" --not --remotes)\n      if [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 ]] && git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" cat-file -e \"${remote_sha}^{commit}\" 2>/dev/null; then\n        _id_range=(\"$local_sha\" --not \"$remote_sha\" --remotes)\n      fi\n      _id_log=\"\"\n      if ! _id_log=\"$(git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" log --no-merges \\\n           --format='%h%x09%(trailers:key=Commit-Id,valueonly,separator=%x2C)%x09%s' \"${_id_range[@]}\")\"; then\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:commit-id]: the commits pushed to '$branch_name' could not be read; git's error is above.\" >&2\n        REJECTED=1\n        continue\n      fi\n      _id_missing=\"$(printf '%s\\n' \"$_id_log\" \\\n        | awk -F'\\t' 'NF && ($2 !~ /^[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]$/ || $2 == \"0000000\") { print \"   \" $1 \" \" $3 }')\"\n      if [[ -n \"$_id_missing\" ]]; then\n        {\n          echo \"BLOCKED [gate:commit-id]: commits pushed to '$branch_name' carry no Commit-Id trailer:\"\n          printf '%s\\n' \"$_id_missing\" | head -n 5\n          echo \"   ($(printf '%s\\n' \"$_id_missing\" | wc -l | tr -d ' ') in all)\"\n          echo \"   Stamp them by re-committing each, which runs the stamper:\"\n          echo \"     git rebase -x 'git commit --amend --no-edit' <the commit before the first one>\"\n          echo \"   To exempt this repository: git config --local skilled.commitId false\"\n        } >&2\n        REJECTED=1\n        continue\n      fi\n    fi\n  fi\n\n  # Release branches are the one namespace the naming/permission gates never touch.\n  case \"$branch_name\" in\n    skilled/v*) continue ;;\n  esac\n\n  # Naming gate removed: it never rejected a push the permission gate below would\n  # have allowed, and that gate is what asks a human before a new branch reaches\n  # origin. A wrapper ref is caught there too, as a new branch.\n\n  # ── Gate 2: remote-push permission (every push, new or update) ──\n  if [[ \"$NAMING_AVAILABLE\" -eq 0 ]]; then\n    # Approval keeps its usual meaning: `1` passes an update, the branch name passes\n    # any push, and the live autosync branch keeps its exception.\n    if [[ \"$NAMING_MISSING\" -eq 1 && \"$is_autosync_live\" -eq 0 \\\n          && \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH:-0}\" != \"$branch_name\" ]] \\\n       && ! [[ \"$is_new\" -eq 0 && \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH:-0}\" = \"1\" ]]; then\n      {\n        echo \"\"\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:remote-permission]: the permission script is missing: $NAMING\"\n        echo \"Without it the remote allowlist cannot be read, so this push needs explicit approval:\"\n        echo \"  SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=1 git push ...   (an update)\"\n        echo \"  SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=$branch_name git push ...   (creating the branch)\"\n        echo \"\"\n      } >&2\n      REJECTED=1\n    fi\n    continue\n  fi\n  # Creating a remote branch is the act that outlives the session: an update can\n  # be reset, but a branch nobody meant to publish stays until someone notices.\n  # The environment bypass authorises updates only — a new remote branch needs\n  # the allowlist, which is a file a person edits.\n  if [[ \"$remote_sha\" =~ ^0+$ ]] \\\n     && ! is_remote_push_allowlisted \"$branch_name\" \\\n     && [[ \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH:-0}\" != \"$branch_name\" ]]; then\n    {\n      echo \"\"\n      echo \"BLOCKED [gate:remote-create]: '$branch_name' does not exist on origin, and creating it is not something a session may decide.\"\n      echo \"Branches stay local unless a person approves publishing them.\"\n      echo \"To approve permanently, add the branch to:\"\n      echo \"  .opencode/skills/sk-git/scripts/remote-branch-allowlist.txt\"\n      echo \"For a one-off, name the branch rather than using the blanket switch:\"\n      echo \"  SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=$branch_name git push ...\"\n      echo \"A bare SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=1 authorises updates, never creation.\"\n      echo \"\"\n    } >&2\n    REJECTED=1\n    continue\n  fi\n  # `1` approves this push; naming the branch approves it and permits creating\n  # it. Both are per-push acts by whoever runs the command.\n  if [[ \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH:-0}\" = \"1\" || \"${SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH:-0}\" = \"$branch_name\" ]]; then\n    continue\n  fi\n  if is_remote_push_allowlisted \"$branch_name\"; then\n    continue\n  fi\n  # Continuous-integration autosync exception, scoped to the exact live\n  # branch: git-sync.sh's own contract never asks the caller mid-hook (see\n  # references/continuous-integration.md), and the live branch was already an\n  # explicit operator choice (the primary checkout's own branch) before any\n  # session started publishing to it. Any OTHER branch still asks, even with\n  # SPECKIT_AUTOSYNC=1 set — this is not a blanket autosync bypass.\n  if [[ \"$is_autosync_live\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n    continue\n  fi\n  {\n    echo \"\"\n    echo \"BLOCKED [gate:remote-permission]: push to '$branch_name' on origin needs explicit permission for this push.\"\n    echo \"This repo asks before every push to a branch outside the remote allowlist\"\n    echo \"(built in: main, skilled/v*; extend via\"\n    echo \"  .opencode/skills/sk-git/scripts/remote-branch-allowlist.txt).\"\n    echo \"If the operator has approved THIS push, retry with:\"\n    echo \"  SPECKIT_ALLOW_REMOTE_PUSH=1 git push ...\"\n    echo \"\"\n  } >&2\n  REJECTED=1\ndone\n\nif [[ \"$REJECTED\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n  exit 1\nfi\n\n# ── Skill-metadata gate (independently bypassable) ───────────────────────────\n#\n# Runs the fleet-wide skill-root metadata class gate before skill changes reach\n# origin. It exists because both live regressions to date were pushes that grew\n# a skill's doc corpus without regenerating its generated files — CI on the\n# release line only catches that after the fact, while this catches it before.\n# The gate is dependency-free and finishes in about a second. It warns and never\n# blocks: CI enforces the same check on main and skilled/v*, and the hook cannot\n# regenerate bytes for a commit that already exists. A stale result is fixed with\n# `ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs --fix`.\n#\n# The gate reads the working tree, which on a normal push from a clean worktree\n# is exactly what is being pushed. A deliberately partial push from a dirty\n# tree can skip with SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_SKILL_GATE=1.\nif [[ \"${SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_SKILL_GATE:-0}\" != \"1\" ]]; then\n  SKILL_GATE=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs\"\n  # Per-ref ranges avoid coupling a live-branch publish to the wrapper branch's\n  # upstream configuration. New remote branches still defer to CI because they\n  # have no prior remote tree to compare without scanning an unrelated history.\n  if [[ \"$SKILL_CHANGES_PUSHED\" -eq 1 && ! -f \"$SKILL_GATE\" ]]; then\n    # Only a checkout that ships the toolchain has a checker to miss.\n    if _in_toolchain_repo; then\n      echo \"WARNING [gate:skill-root-metadata]: checker is missing: $SKILL_GATE; push gate failed open.\" >&2\n    fi\n  elif [[ -f \"$SKILL_GATE\" && \"$SKILL_CHANGES_PUSHED\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n    if ! node \"$SKILL_GATE\" >&2; then\n      {\n        echo \"\"\n        if [[ \"$AUTOSYNC_LIVE_PUSH\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n          echo \"WARNING [gate:skill-root-metadata]: this live-branch publish carries stale skill metadata.\"\n          echo \"Not blocking: the hook cannot regenerate committed bytes, and CI enforces this on main and skilled/v*.\"\n        else\n          echo \"WARNING [gate:skill-root-metadata]: skill-root metadata is stale for skills being pushed.\"\n          echo \"Not blocking: CI enforces this on every push to main and skilled/v*.\"\n        fi\n        echo \"Fix with:  node .opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs --fix\"\n        echo \"\"\n      } >&2\n    fi\n  fi\nfi\n\n# ── Compiled-routing gate (third gate, independently bypassable) ─────────────\n# Editing a hub's routing inputs invalidates its minted manifest, which silently\n# drops that hub to legacy routing. Nothing errors locally and nothing logs, so it\n# was only ever found after the push, as a failed CI mail — this one check produced\n# the large majority of them. It is dependency-free and runs in a fraction of a\n# second, so it belongs here where it can be acted on rather than after the fact.\n#\n# The test suites that used to run here moved to CI. They were report-only, so the\n# forty seconds they cost every push bought nothing that could block, and their\n# output was filtered too narrowly to say what had failed.\n#\n# The routing guard below does block, because the failure it catches is silent\n# everywhere else: a stale pin makes a hub fall back to serving legacy, every\n# recommendation loses its compiled route, and no error is raised anywhere. The\n# continuous integration job that was supposed to catch it could not see a nested\n# packet file for months, so relying on it alone is what let this stand twice.\n# Skip with SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE=1.\nif [[ \"${SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE:-0}\" != \"1\" ]]; then\n  ROUTE_GUARD=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/bin/compiled-route-guard.cjs\"\n  if [[ ! -f \"$ROUTE_GUARD\" ]] && _in_toolchain_repo; then\n    echo \"\" >&2\n    echo \"BLOCKED [gate:compiled-routing]: route guard is missing: $ROUTE_GUARD\" >&2\n    echo \"Override once, deliberately: SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE=1 git push ...\" >&2\n    exit 1\n  fi\n  if [[ -f \"$ROUTE_GUARD\" ]]; then\n    if ! node \"$ROUTE_GUARD\" >&2; then\n      {\n        echo \"\"\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:compiled-routing]: a hub's routing manifest is stale or drifted.\"\n        echo \"A stale pin makes the hub serve legacy, which silently drops the compiled\"\n        echo \"route from every recommendation. Nothing else reports it, and it has twice\"\n        echo \"stood for several commits here, which is why this now blocks rather than warns.\"\n        echo \"Fix: refresh the hub, then resync its authored copy in the same change —\"\n        echo \"     refreshing alone leaves the runtime and authored manifests disagreeing.\"\n        echo \"     Then rebuild and re-pin that hub's canary before pushing.\"\n        echo \"Override once, deliberately: SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE=1 git push ...\"\n        echo \"\"\n      } >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n  fi\n\n  # The guard above reads the WORKING TREE. That is the tree the author can fix, so\n  # it is the right thing to validate -- but it is not the tree that reaches anyone\n  # else. When a commit carries minted manifests whose source edits are still\n  # uncommitted, the working tree is coherent and the published commit is not: a\n  # fresh clone recomputes different hashes and those hubs fall back to legacy.\n  # That has happened here, and neither this gate nor the commit-time one saw it,\n  # because the commit gate keys on staged inputs and manifests alone do not trigger\n  # it. So assert the pushed commit carries the same routing bytes the guard just\n  # approved. Anything git cannot resolve is skipped rather than blocked: a hook\n  # that guesses is worse than one that misses.\n  ROUTING_BYTES=(\n    '.opencode/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/*/manifest.json'\n    '.opencode/skills/*/hub-router.json'\n    '.opencode/skills/*/mode-registry.json'\n    '.opencode/skills/*/leaf-manifest.json'\n    '.opencode/skills/*/ROUTER.md'\n    '.opencode/skills/*/SKILL.md'\n    '.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/*/manifest.json'\n    '.skilled/skills/*/hub-router.json'\n    '.skilled/skills/*/mode-registry.json'\n    '.skilled/skills/*/leaf-manifest.json'\n    '.skilled/skills/*/ROUTER.md'\n    '.skilled/skills/*/SKILL.md'\n  )\n  # The comparison checks the guard's own verdict, so it runs only where the guard ran,\n  # and only for the pushed commit the working tree belongs to: any other commit would be\n  # compared with a tree it never had.\n  HEAD_SHA=\"$(git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" rev-parse -q --verify HEAD 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n  for pushed_sha in $PUSHED_SHAS; do\n    [[ -f \"$ROUTE_GUARD\" && -n \"$HEAD_SHA\" && \"$pushed_sha\" == \"$HEAD_SHA\" ]] || continue\n    git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" cat-file -e \"${pushed_sha}^{commit}\" 2>/dev/null || continue\n    if ! git_bounded -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" diff --quiet \"$pushed_sha\" -- \"${ROUTING_BYTES[@]}\" 2>/dev/null; then\n      {\n        echo \"\"\n        echo \"BLOCKED [gate:routing-commit-parity]: the routing bytes you are pushing are\"\n        echo \"not the ones just validated. The guard passed against your working tree, and\"\n        echo \"commit ${pushed_sha:0:10} differs from it in a routing input or a minted manifest.\"\n        echo \"A clone of that commit recomputes different policy hashes and serves legacy.\"\n        echo \"Fix: commit the routing sources and manifests together, then push again.\"\n        echo \"See what differs:\"\n        echo \"  git diff ${pushed_sha:0:10} -- '.skilled/skills/*/hub-router.json' \\\\\"\n        echo \"      '.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation/*/manifest.json'\"\n        echo \"Override once, deliberately: SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_ROUTE_GATE=1 git push ...\"\n        echo \"\"\n      } >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n  done\nfi\n\n# ── Track-root gate (independently bypassable) ──────────────────────────────\n# A track root lists its packets in children_ids, and no packet rule ever reads\n# that list, so it drifted in most tracks with nothing reporting it. create.sh\n# --track keeps the list current for each new packet; this blocks a commit that\n# would publish a list and a set of packets that disagree. It reads each pushed\n# commit, not the working tree, because a shared checkout carries other sessions'\n# unfinished packets. A symlinked track holds another repository's files, so the\n# sweep leaves it out. The sweep is dependency-free and runs in well under a second.\n# Skip with SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_TRACK_GATE=1.\nif [[ \"${SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_TRACK_GATE:-0}\" != \"1\" ]] && _in_toolchain_repo && [[ -d \"$REPO_ROOT/specs\" ]]; then\n  TRACK_SWEEP=\"$SOURCE_ROOT/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/sweep-track-roots.mjs\"\n  if [[ ! -f \"$TRACK_SWEEP\" ]]; then\n    echo \"\" >&2\n    echo \"BLOCKED [gate:track-roots]: sweep is missing: $TRACK_SWEEP\" >&2\n    echo \"Override once, deliberately: SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_TRACK_GATE=1 git push ...\" >&2\n    exit 1\n  else\n    for pushed_sha in $PUSHED_SHAS; do\n      git -C \"$REPO_ROOT\" cat-file -e \"${pushed_sha}^{commit}\" 2>/dev/null || continue\n      _track_rc=0\n      _track_out=\"$(node \"$TRACK_SWEEP\" --specs \"$REPO_ROOT/specs\" --rev \"$pushed_sha\" 2>&1)\" || _track_rc=$?\n      if [[ \"$_track_rc\" -eq 1 ]]; then\n        {\n          echo \"\"\n          echo \"BLOCKED [gate:track-roots]: in commit ${pushed_sha:0:10}, a track root's children_ids\"\n          echo \"do not list exactly the packets that track holds:\"\n          echo \"$_track_out\" | sed 's/^/  /'\n          echo \"Fix: node $(dirname \"${TRACK_SWEEP#\"$REPO_ROOT\"/}\")/refresh-track-roots.mjs --apply\"\n          echo \"     then commit each changed graph-metadata.json together with the packets it\"\n          echo \"     lists, and push again.\"\n          echo \"Override once, deliberately: SPECKIT_SKIP_PREPUSH_TRACK_GATE=1 git push ...\"\n          echo \"\"\n        } >&2\n        exit 1\n      elif [[ \"$_track_rc\" -ne 0 ]]; then\n        echo \"WARNING [gate:track-roots]: could not sweep commit ${pushed_sha:0:10} (exit $_track_rc); push gate failed open.\" >&2\n        echo \"$_track_out\" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2\n      fi\n    done\n  fi\nfi\n\nexit 0\n\n\n[showing lines 719-1680 of 1680; full output saved to /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/opencode/shell/4c10847297631c3af43d766cf1908920fe00c449/sh_1ceaccd46001kS7dOHs1GL6EYl.out]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled && ls -la .skilled/scripts/git-hooks/ && echo \\\"=== pre-commit\\\" && cat .skilled/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit && echo \\\"=== pre-push\\\" && cat .skilled/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push\"}"},"time":{"start":1794468793024,"end":1794468793698}}}}
